      "generated_answer": "In her speech, Eleanor Roosevelt notes that \"it is impossible, of course, to be at war and keep freedom of the press,\" referring to the historical precedent of civil liberties being curtailed during times of conflict. Given the speech was delivered in 1940, she is alluding to the domestic restrictions on speech and assembly experienced during World War I (such as the Espionage and Sedition Acts). The other listed wars occurred either too far in the past or after the date of this address.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
