    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4538",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Genetic studies of the two primary varieties of rice, japonica (short-grained), and indica (long-grained), suggest that:\n\nOptions: \nA. each is more closely related to one another than their local wild ancestors, suggesting that these modern varieties of rice were developed recently in east Asia.\nB. each is more closely related to local wild ancestors than one another, suggesting that these modern varieties of rice were domesticated separately in east Asia and south Asia.\nC. each is unrelated to one another, suggesting that these modern varieties of rice were domesticated independently in east Asia and western Europe.\nD. each is closely related to their local wild ancestors, suggesting that domestication must have occurred very recently and no earlier than 2,000 years ago.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Genetic studies have shown that *japonica* and *indica* rice varieties evolved from distinct wild populations in different geographical regions, specifically East Asia and South Asia respectively. This evidence supports the theory of independent domestication events rather than a single point of origin. Consequently, they share a closer genetic relationship with their respective local wild ancestors than they do with each other.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
