    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4622",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"The only force which is strong enough to break down social convention is economic necessity. . . . The economic necessity which has forced women out of the home and into the world of business has completely annihilated the old idea that a woman should eat only in the privacy of her household or in the homes of her friends, has created the absolutely new social phenomenon of women eating in public, unescorted by men, by the tens of thousands, and has given rise to a wholly new phase of the restaurant business.\"\n—The New York Times, October 15, 1905\nThe scenario described in the passage above is most directly reflected in the ideas of which of the following?\n\nOptions: \nA. Civil Rights Act of 1964.\nB. Pure Food and Drug Act.\nC. The Social Security Act\nD. The Glass-Steagall Act\nE. The 19th Amendment\nF. The Emancipation Proclamation\nG. The Americans with Disabilities Act\nH. Interstate Commerce Act.\nI. Equal Rights Amendment.\nJ. The Fair Labor Standards Act\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The passage describes the shift of women into the public sphere and the workforce, highlighting the changing social status and economic independence of women at the turn of the 20th century. This movement toward female autonomy and presence in public life is most directly reflected in the **19th Amendment**, which granted women the right to vote and represented the culmination of their increasing social and political influence. Other options relate to labor, civil rights, or economic regulation that do not specifically address the social transition of women described.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
