    "global index": "MMLUPro_history_4842",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: This question refers to the following information.\n\"An act for the more effectual protection of the property of married women:\n\"§1. The real property of any female who may hereafter marry, and which she shall own at the time of marriage, and the rents, issues, and profits thereof, shall not be subject to the sole disposal of her husband, nor be liable for his debts, and shall continue her sole and separate property, as if she were a single female.\n\"§2. The real and personal property, and the rents, issues, and profits thereof, of any female now married, shall not be subject to the disposal of her husband; but shall be her sole and separate property, as if she were a single female, except so far as the same may be liable for the debts of her husband heretofore contracted.\n\"§3. Any married female may take by inheritance, or by gift, grant, devise, or bequest, from any person other than her husband, and hold to her sole and separate use, and convey and devise real and personal property, and any interest or estate therein, and the rents, issues, and profits thereof, in the same manner and with like effect as if she were unmarried, and the same shall not be subject to the disposal of her husband nor be liable for his debts.\"\n—Married Women's Property Act, New York State (1848)\nWhich of the following groups would be most likely to support the perspective of the Married Women's Property Act?\n\nOptions: \nA. Congregational ministers.\nB. Members of the American Anti-Slavery Society.\nC. Supporters of the \"separate spheres\" ideology.\nD. Southern supporters of the concept of \"female virtue.\"\nE. Proponents of the \"cult of domesticity\" value system.\nF. Supporters of the \"true womanhood\" ideal.\nG. Opponents of the women's suffrage movement.\nH. Advocates for the patriarchal family structure.\nI. Participants in the Seneca Falls Convention.\nJ. Advocates for the traditional gender roles in society.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "gemini-3-flash-preview",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The Married Women's Property Act of 1848 was a landmark piece of legislation that granted married women greater legal control over their property, aligning with the early women's rights movement. Participants in the Seneca Falls Convention, held that same year, were primary advocates for such legal reforms to dismantle the status of *coverture* and secure economic independence for women. In contrast, the other options represent groups or ideologies that generally favored traditional, restricted roles for women within the domestic sphere.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
