    "global index": "LiveCodeBench_344",
    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nAtCoder Inc. sells T-shirts with its logo.\nYou are given Takahashi's schedule for N days as a string S of length N consisting of 0, 1, and 2.\nSpecifically, for an integer i satisfying 1\\leq i\\leq N,\n\n- if the i-th character of S is 0, he has no plan scheduled for the i-th day;\n- if the i-th character of S is 1, he plans to go out for a meal on the i-th day;\n- if the i-th character of S is 2, he plans to attend a competitive programming event on the i-th day.\n\nTakahashi has M plain T-shirts, all washed and ready to wear just before the first day.\nIn addition, to be able to satisfy the following conditions, he will buy several AtCoder logo T-shirts.\n\n- On days he goes out for a meal, he will wear a plain or logo T-shirt.\n- On days he attends a competitive programming event, he will wear a logo T-shirt.\n- On days with no plans, he will not wear any T-shirts. Also, he will wash all T-shirts worn at that point. He can wear them again from the next day onwards.\n- Once he wears a T-shirt, he cannot wear it again until he washes it.\n\nDetermine the minimum number of T-shirts he needs to buy to be able to wear appropriate T-shirts on all scheduled days during the N days. If he does not need to buy new T-shirts, print 0.\nAssume that the purchased T-shirts are also washed and ready to use just before the first day.\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\nS\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the minimum number of T-shirts Takahashi needs to buy to be able to satisfy the conditions in the problem statement.\nIf he does not need to buy new T-shirts, print 0.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 1\\leq M\\leq N\\leq 1000\n- S is a string of length N consisting of 0, 1, and 2.\n- N and M are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n6 1\n112022\n\nSample Output 1\n\n2\n\nIf Takahashi buys two logo T-shirts, he can wear T-shirts as follows:\n\n- On the first day, he wears a logo T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the second day, he wears a plain T-shirt to go out for a meal.\n- On the third day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the fourth day, he has no plans, so he washes all the worn T-shirts. This allows him to reuse the T-shirts worn on the first, second, and third days.\n- On the fifth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n- On the sixth day, he wears a logo T-shirt to attend a competitive programming event.\n\nIf he buys one or fewer logo T-shirts, he cannot use T-shirts to meet the conditions no matter what. Hence, print 2.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n3 1\n222\n\nSample Output 2\n\n3\n\nSample Input 3\n\n2 1\n01\n\nSample Output 3\n\n0\n\nHe does not need to buy new T-shirts.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef solve():\n    import sys\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    N, M = map(int, data[0].split())\n    S = data[1].strip()\n    \n    # We'll simulate day by day, keeping track of available plain shirts and logo shirts\n    # plain_shirts: number of plain shirts available (washed and ready to wear)\n    # logo_shirts: number of logo shirts available (washed and ready to wear)\n    # We'll also track how many logo shirts we've bought (initially 0)\n    \n    # Before day 1: we have M plain shirts and some logo shirts (that we'll decide to buy)\n    # We'll use a greedy approach with binary search on the number of logo shirts to buy\n    \n    # Instead, let's think: we need to find minimum logo shirts to buy\n    # We can try to simulate with k logo shirts and check if it's enough\n    \n    # But constraints are small (N <= 1000), so we can simulate greedily day by day\n    # and keep track of needed logo shirts\n    \n    # Key insight: On days with '1' (meal), we can use either plain or logo shirt\n    # On days with '2' (event), we must use logo shirt\n    # On days with '0' (no plan), we wash all shirts -> all shirts become available again\n    \n    # We'll simulate day by day, keeping track of:\n    # - how many plain shirts are currently available (washed and not worn since last wash)\n    # - how many logo shirts are currently available (washed and not worn since last wash)\n    \n    # Initially, before any day: plain_available = M, logo_available = k (what we bought)\n    # But we don't know k yet. So instead, let's track the minimum k needed.\n    \n    # Alternative approach: \n    # The problem reduces to: in each \"segment\" (consecutive days between '0's, including before first '0' and after last '0'),\n    # we need to handle the days with '2' (must use logo) and days with '1' (can use either).\n    # \n    # For each segment, let:\n    #   count1 = number of '1's in the segment\n    #   count2 = number of '2's in the segment\n    # \n    # In this segment, we start with some number of plain shirts (from previous wash) and logo shirts (from previous wash).\n    # Since at the start of a segment, all shirts are washed (due to the preceding '0' or because it's the beginning),\n    # we have M plain shirts and (bought_logo) logo shirts available at the start of each segment.\n    # \n    # However, plain shirts are cheaper: they are only used for '1' days (since '2' days require logo).\n    # So we should use plain shirts first for '1' days, then use logo shirts for remaining '1' days and all '2' days.\n    #\n    # For a segment, the total logo shirts needed in that segment is: max(0, count2 + max(0, count1 - M))\n    # Explanation: We use M plain shirts for up to M '1's, so if count1 > M, we need (count1 - M) logo shirts for the remaining '1's,\n    # plus we need count2 logo shirts for the '2' days.\n    #\n    # But wait: the same logo shirt can be reused *only* after a '0' day (since washing happens only on '0' days).\n    # So within a segment (between two '0's), each day requires a *distinct* shirt (because no washing happens in between).\n    # Therefore, the above formula is correct: for each segment, we need (count1 + count2) shirts in total,\n    # where plain shirts (M) can cover up to M days of type '1', and the rest must be covered by logo shirts.\n    #\n    # However, note: plain shirts can't be used for '2' days. So the logo shirts needed for a segment is:\n    #   max(0, count2 + max(0, count1 - M)) = max(count2, count1 + count2 - M)\n    # But actually: total required shirts in segment = count1 + count2, \n    # and plain shirts can cover at most min(M, count1) days (only the '1's), so logo shirts needed = count1 + count2 - min(M, count1) = max(count1 + count2 - M, count2)\n    # However, note: if count1 + count2 <= M, then we don't need any logo shirts for this segment.\n    # So logo shirts needed for segment = max(0, count1 + count2 - M)\n    #\n    # But wait: is that correct? Let's see:\n    # - If count1 = 3, count2 = 2, M = 3: then total days = 5, plain shirts cover 3 (the '1's), so we need 2 logo shirts (for the '2's). \n    #   Formula: max(0, 3+2 - 3) = 2 -> correct.\n    # - If count1 = 3, count2 = 2, M = 4: then plain shirts cover all 3 '1's, and we still need 2 logo shirts for '2's.\n    #   Formula: max(0, 5-4) = 1 -> incorrect! \n    #\n    # The mistake: plain shirts can't be used for '2' days. So the logo shirts must cover all '2' days and the '1' days beyond M.\n    # Therefore, logo shirts needed for segment = count2 + max(0, count1 - M)\n    #\n    # Example: count1=3, count2=2, M=4 -> 2 + max(0, 3-4) = 2 + 0 = 2 -> correct.\n    # Example: count1=5, count2=2, M=3 -> 2 + max(0,5-3) = 2+2=4 -> total 5+2=7 days, 3 plain cover 3 '1's, so 2 '1's and 2 '2's need logo -> 4, correct.\n    #\n    # So for a segment, logo_shirts_needed = count2 + max(0, count1 - M)\n    #\n    # Now, the same logo shirts can be reused across segments (because washing on '0' days makes all shirts available again).\n    # So the total logo shirts we need to buy is the *maximum* over all segments of the logo_shirts_needed for that segment.\n    # Why? Because after a '0' day, we get all shirts back, so the limiting factor is the segment that requires the most logo shirts.\n    #\n    # Example from Sample Input 1: \"112022\", N=6, M=1\n    # Segment 1: \"112\" -> count1=2, count2=1 -> logo needed = 1 + max(0,2-1) = 1+1=2\n    # Segment 2: \"22\" -> count1=0, count2=2 -> logo needed = 2 + max(0,0-1)=2+0=2\n    # So max(2,2)=2 -> buy 2 logo shirts.\n    #\n    # Sample Input 2: \"222\", M=1\n    # One segment: count1=0, count2=3 -> logo needed = 3 + max(0,0-1)=3\n    #\n    # Sample Input 3: \"01\", M=1\n    # Segment 1: before first '0'? Actually, the string is \"01\":\n    #   Day1: '0' -> washes all shirts, but no shirt worn that day.\n    #   Day2: '1' -> count1=1, count2=0\n    # Segment for day2: count1=1, count2=0 -> logo needed = 0 + max(0,1-1)=0\n    # So we don't need any logo shirts.\n    #\n    # However, note: the segments are separated by '0's. We need to split the string by '0's, but also consider segments at the beginning and end.\n    # Steps:\n    #   1. Split S by '0' -> we get a list of segments (each segment is a string of '1's and '2's)\n    #   2. For each segment, count count1 (number of '1') and count2 (number of '2')\n    #   3. For each segment, compute needed = count2 + max(0, count1 - M)\n    #   4. Answer = max(needed over all segments, 0) [but if no segment, then 0]\n    \n    segments = S.split('0')\n    \n    max_needed = 0\n    for seg in segments:\n        count1 = seg.count('1')\n        count2 = seg.count('2')\n        needed = count2 + max(0, count1 - M)\n        if needed > max_needed:\n            max_needed = needed\n    \n    print(max_needed)\n\nsolve()\n```",
