      "generated_answer": "```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    edges = []\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        edges.append((u, v))\n    \n    K = int(next(it))\n    forbidden_pairs = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        forbidden_pairs.append((x, y))\n    \n    Q = int(next(it))\n    queries = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q = int(next(it))\n        queries.append((p, q))\n    \n    # Build initial graph (ignoring self-loops and multi-edges for connectivity)\n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx != ry:\n            parent[rx] = ry\n    \n    # Initialize union-find with M edges (including duplicates and self-loops)\n    for u, v in edges:\n        if u == v:  # self-loop, skip for connectivity\n            continue\n        union(u, v)\n    \n    # Now check for each query\n    results = []\n    for p, q in queries:\n        # Temporarily add edge (p, q)\n        rp = find(p)\n        rq = find(q)\n        \n        # Check if adding this edge connects any forbidden pair\n        still_good = True\n        for x, y in forbidden_pairs:\n            rx_orig = find(x)\n            ry_orig = find(y)\n            # If x and y were already connected, then the original graph violates the condition?\n            # But the problem says \"The given graph G is good\", so this shouldn't happen.\n            if rx_orig == ry_orig:\n                # already connected -> violates condition\n                # But since G is good, this should not be possible.\n                pass # just to clarify logic\n        \n        # After adding edge (p,q), new component connections form\n        # So all nodes in the merged component become connected\n        new_component = None\n        if rp != rq:\n            new_component = min(rp, rq)\n        else:\n            new_component = rp\n        \n        # Instead: we simulate — in the new graph, p and q are connected\n        # So we need to check if any forbidden pair (x, y) becomes connected due to this new edge.\n        for x, y in forbidden_pairs:\n            fx = find(x)\n            fy = find(y)\n            # Without the new edge, x and y are not connected -> fx != fy\n            # With the new edge, x and y may become connected only if the new edge creates a bridge\n            # between the components of x and y\n            \n            # Check: if x and y were in different components originally,\n            # and the new edge connects those two components?\n            if fx != fy:\n                # Will they be connected after adding (p,q)?\n                # Only if (fx,fy) bridge is formed via (p,q)\n                # i.e., one of fx,fy is in p's component and the other in q's?\n                comp_x = fx\n                comp_y = fy\n                comp_p = find(p)\n                comp_q = find(q)\n                \n                if (comp_x == comp_p and comp_y == comp_q) or (comp_x == comp_q and comp_y == comp_p):\n                    # Then x and y become connected via the new edge\n                    still_good = False\n                    break\n            # else: they were already in same component — but original graph is good => cannot be connected\n            # So this case should not occur in a valid input\n        else:\n            # None of the forbidden pairs became connected? Then still good.\n            # But wait: what if multiple connections? We need a better way.\n            # Let's instead rebuild connectivity after union\n            temp_parent = parent[:]\n            def temp_find(x):\n                stack = []\n                while temp_parent[x] != x:\n                    stack.append(x)\n                    x = temp_parent[x]\n                root = x\n                for node in stack:\n                    temp_parent[node] = root\n                return root\n            \n            # Simulate union of p and q\n            r_p = temp_find(p)\n            r_q = temp_find(q)\n            if r_p != r_q:\n                temp_parent[r_p] = r_q\n            # Now check all forbidden pairs\n            for x, y in forbidden_pairs:\n                if temp_find(x) == temp_find(y):\n                    still_good = False\n                    break\n        \n        results.append(\"Yes\" if still_good else \"No\")\n    \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nmain()\n```\n\nWait! There's a flaw in the above code: we are doing a full simulation for each query and resetting parent per query would not scale. Also, creating deep copies during each query is expensive. Let's correct this with a more efficient approach.\n\nWe need to precompute connected components of the original graph, then for each query, simulate adding the edge and check if any forbidden pair ends up in the same component.\n\nBut we can do better:\n\nI'll use union-find on the original graph first, then for each query, we virtually merge two components and check if any forbidden pair gets connected.\n\nBut since checking all K forbidden pairs per query is too slow (Q, K up to 200k), we need optimization.\n\nAlternate idea:\n- Precompute connected components of G using union-find.\n- For each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), they must be in different components. Store each component's id.\n- For a query (p, q): let Cp = comp[p], Cq = comp[q]\n  - If Cp == Cq: adding (p,q) doesn't change connectivity\n  - Else: merging Cp and Cq into one component\n    - Now any forbidden pair (x,y) where x in Cp, y in Cq (or vice versa) would become connected -> bad\n- So we must check: does there exist a forbidden pair (x,y) such that x in Cp and y in Cq?\n\nBut storing all such dependencies is complex.\n\nInstead, we can pre-index forbidden pairs per component pair.\n\nBut components can merge — and we have many queries.\n\nBetter: do nothing on queries; instead, use DSU with rollbacks? Not worth it.\n\nAlternatively, since we only care about whether merging two components introduces a forbidden connection, we can precompute:\n\nLet’s assign a root component to each node.\n\nWe can compress the components: map original component id to smaller id.\n\nThen, for each unordered pair (a, b) of components, we can store whether (a,b) is a forbidden connection — but wait, no: the forbidden pair is between nodes, not components.\n\nActually, each forbidden pair (x,y) corresponds to (comp_x, comp_y). If comp_x != comp_y, the pair is allowed (since originally disconnected).\n\nWhen we merge two components C1 and C2, then any forbidden pair (x,y) where x in C1, y in C2 becomes connected.\n\nSo for each query (p,q):\n- if comp[p] == comp[q], then adding (p,q) doesn't change connectivity → answer is \"Yes\" if no forbidden pair exists within merged component? But components weren’t merged before → it's safe?\n  - Actually, if they're already in the same component, then the edge doesn't change anything → so G(i) has same connectivity as G → still good → \"Yes\"\n- else:\n  - Let c1 = comp[p], c2 = comp[q]\n  - Check if there exists any forbidden pair (x,y) such that (comp[x], comp[y]) == (c1, c2) or (c2, c1)\n  - But we must check both directions.\n\nWe can precompute a set: for each unordered component pair (min(c1,c2), max(c1,c2)) that appears in a forbidden pair, we store it.\n\nBut: a single forbidden pair (x,y) gives (cx, cy). But multiple forbidden pairs might exist.\n\nBut if we merge c1 and c2, we don't care how many; one is enough to invalidate.\n\nSo:\n- Precompute component for each node.\n- Map each node to comp id (representative)\n- Create a set: `forbidden_component_pairs = set()`\n  - For each (x,y) in forbidden_pairs:\n    - cx = find(x), cy = find(y)\n    - (Note: cx != cy because G is good)\n    - Add tuple (min(cx,cy), max(cx,cy)) to the set\n\nBut wait: this records only whether the component pair is involved in *any* forbidden pair.\n\nBut when merging, only one such pair causing a path violates the \"good\" condition.\n\nSo for a query:\n- c1 = find(p), c2 = find(q)\n- if c1 == c2: print \"Yes\"\n- else:\n  - a = min(c1, c2); b = max(c1, c2)\n  - if (a, b) in forbidden_component_pairs:\n        print \"No\"\n  - else:\n        print \"Yes\"\n\nBut wait: this is incorrect.\n\nBecause: when we add edge (p,q), we are connecting c1 and c2. But does that directly make every x in c1 connected to every y in c2? Yes.\n\nSo if there was a forbidden pair (x,y) where x in c1 and y in c2, then in the new graph, x and y are connected.\n\nHence: we want to know if there exists any forbidden pair (x,y) such that x's component is c1, y's component is c2.\n\nSo our set `forbidden_component_pairs` must contain exactly such pairs.\n\nSo yes, this logic works.\n\nBut note: multiple distinct forbidden pairs might be between same component pair, but we only need to record existence.\n\nSo here is the correct version:\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef main():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it)); M = int(next(it))\n    \n    edges = []\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(next(it)); v = int(next(it))\n        edges.append((u, v))\n    \n    K = int(next(it))\n    forbidden_pairs = []\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(next(it)); y = int(next(it))\n        forbidden_pairs.append((x, y))\n    \n    Q = int(next(it))\n    queries = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(next(it)); q = int(next(it))\n        queries.append((p, q))\n    \n    # DSU for original graph\n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    \n    def find(x):\n        if parent[x] != x:\n            parent[x] = find(parent[x])\n        return parent[x]\n    \n    def union(x, y):\n        rx, ry = find(x), find(y)\n        if rx != ry:\n            if rx < ry:\n                parent[ry] = rx\n            else:\n                parent[rx] = ry\n    \n    for u, v in edges:\n        if u != v:  # skip self-loops\n            union(u, v)\n    \n    # Find the component (root) for each node\n    comp = [0] * (N + 1)\n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        comp[i] = find(i)\n    \n    # Build set of forbidden component pairs\n    forbidden_component_pairs = set()\n    for x, y in forbidden_pairs:\n        cx = comp[x]\n        cy = comp[y]\n        if cx > cy:\n            cx, cy = cy, cx\n        # cx != cy guaranteed, because G is good\n        forbidden_component_pairs.add((cx, cy))\n    \n    # Process each query\n    results = []\n    for p, q in queries:\n        cp = comp[p]\n        cq = comp[q]\n        if cp == cq:\n            # Already in same component, adding edge doesn't change connectivity\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            # Normalize component order\n            if cp > cq:\n                cp, cq = cq, cp\n            if (cp, cq) in forbidden_component_pairs:\n                results.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                results.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    print('\\n'.join(results))\n\nmain()\n```",
