      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step by step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We have a graph G that is initially \"good\" - meaning for K given pairs (x_i, y_i), there's no path between x_i and y_i in G.\n2. For each query, we add an edge (p_i, q_i) and need to check if the resulting graph is still good.\n3. The graph becomes \"not good\" if after adding the edge, there exists some pair (x_j, y_j) such that x_j and y_j are now connected.\n\n## Key Insight\n\n- When we add an edge (p, q), we connect the connected components containing p and q.\n- For the graph to remain good, the new edge should NOT connect any pair (x_i, y_i) that were previously disconnected.\n- In other words, for the graph to remain good, there should be NO pair (x_i, y_i) such that x_i and y_i end up in the same connected component after adding the edge (p, q).\n\n## Algorithm\n\n1. First, build the initial graph and find connected components.\n2. For each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), they must be in different components initially.\n3. When adding edge (p, q), let's say p is in component A and q is in component B.\n   - After adding the edge, components A and B become one component.\n   - The graph is NOT good if there exists any forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) where x_i is in A and y_i is in B (or vice versa).\n4. To efficiently check this, we can:\n   - Assign each component a unique ID.\n   - For each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), note which two components they belong to (comp_x[i], comp_y[i]).\n   - For each query (p, q), get comp_p and comp_q.\n   - Check if there's any forbidden pair where one endpoint is in comp_p and the other in comp_q.\n\n## Optimization\n\nSince K and Q can be up to 2×10^5, we can't check all K pairs for each query.\n\nAlternative approach:\n- Create a mapping: for each pair of components (A, B) where A < B, check if there's any forbidden pair connecting them.\n- But the number of component pairs could be large.\n\nBetter approach:\n- Represent each component by a unique ID.\n- For each forbidden pair (x_i, y_i), let c_x = component[x_i], c_y = component[y_i]. Since the original graph is good, c_x ≠ c_y.\n- Store all forbidden component pairs in a set (as sorted tuples to handle undirected nature).\n- For each query (p, q), get comp_p and comp_q:\n  - If comp_p == comp_q, then adding the edge doesn't change connectivity, so if the original graph was good, it remains good → Yes.\n  - If comp_p ≠ comp_q, then after adding the edge, components comp_p and comp_q merge. The graph becomes not good if there exists a forbidden pair (x_i, y_i) such that {component[x_i], component[y_i]} = {comp_p, comp_q}.\n\nSo we just need to check if the unordered pair (min(comp_p, comp_q), max(comp_p, comp_q)) exists in our set of forbidden component pairs.\n\nLet's implement this:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    input = sys.stdin.read\n    data = input().split()\n    \n    # Parse N, M\n    idx = 0\n    N = int(data[idx])\n    M = int(data[idx + 1])\n    idx += 2\n    \n    # Build adjacency list for the graph\n    adj = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    # Read M edges\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(data[idx])\n        v = int(data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        # Handle self-loops (they don't affect connectivity) and multi-edges\n        if u != v:  # Skip self-loops for union-find, but they don't help connectivity anyway\n            adj[u].append(v)\n            adj[v].append(u)\n    \n    # Find connected components using BFS/DFS\n    visited = [False] * (N + 1)\n    component_id = [0] * (N + 1)\n    comp_count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(1, N + 1):\n        if not visited[i]:\n            comp_count += 1\n            # BFS\n            queue = deque([i])\n            visited[i] = True\n            component_id[i] = comp_count\n            \n            while queue:\n                node = queue.popleft()\n                for neighbor in adj[node]:\n                    if not visited[neighbor]:\n                        visited[neighbor] = True\n                        component_id[neighbor] = comp_count\n                        queue.append(neighbor)\n    \n    # Read K\n    K = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    # Read K forbidden pairs and store the component pairs\n    forbidden_comp_pairs = set()\n    \n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(data[idx])\n        y = int(data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        comp_x = component_id[x]\n        comp_y = component_id[y]\n        # Store as sorted tuple to handle undirected pairs\n        if comp_x > comp_y:\n            comp_x, comp_y = comp_y, comp_x\n        forbidden_comp_pairs.add((comp_x, comp_y))\n    \n    # Read Q\n    Q = int(data[idx])\n    idx += 1\n    \n    # Process each query\n    results = []\n    \n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(data[idx])\n        q = int(data[idx + 1])\n        idx += 2\n        \n        comp_p = component_id[p]\n        comp_q = component_id[q]\n        \n        # If they're already in the same component, adding an edge doesn't change connectivity\n        if comp_p == comp_q:\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n        else:\n            # Create sorted pair\n            if comp_p > comp_q:\n                comp_p, comp_q = comp_q, comp_p\n            # Check if this component pair is forbidden\n            if (comp_p, comp_q) in forbidden_comp_pairs:\n                results.append(\"No\")\n            else:\n                results.append(\"Yes\")\n    \n    # Output results\n    for res in results:\n        print(res)\n\nsolve()\n```",
