y_1 = 5. Therefore, print No.\n- For the second question, the graph G^{{(2)}} is not good because it has a path 2 \\rightarrow 6 connecting vertices x_2 = 2 and y_2 = 6. Therefore, print No.\n- For the third question, the graph G^{{(3)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n- For the fourth question, the graph G^{{(4)}} is good. Therefore, print Yes.\n\nAs seen in this sample input, note that the given graph G may have self-loops or multi-edges.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine if the graph $G^{(i)}$ is \"good\" after adding edge $(p_i, q_i)$, we need to check if any of the $K$ restricted pairs $(x_j, y_j)$ become connected. \n\nAn edge $(p_i, q_i)$ connects $x_j$ and $y_j$ if and only if:\n1. $x_j$ is connected to $p_i$ AND $y_j$ is connected to $q_i$ in the original graph $G$.\n2. $x_j$ is connected to $q_i$ AND $y_j$ is connected to $p_i$ in the original graph $G$.\n\nUsing a Disjoint Set Union (DSU) structure, we can map each vertex to its connected component ID. For each restricted pair $(x_j, y_j)$, we store the pair of component IDs $(root(x_j), root(y_j))$ in a set. Then, for each query $(p_i, q_i)$, we check if $(root(p_i), root(q_i))$ or $(root(q_i), root(p_i))$ exists in that set.\n\n```python\nimport sys\n\ndef solve():\n    # Use fast I/O\n    input = sys.stdin.read().split()\n    if not input:\n        return\n    \n    idx = 0\n    N = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    M = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    \n    parent = list(range(N + 1))\n    def find(i):\n        if parent[i] == i:\n            return i\n        parent[i] = find(parent[i])\n        return parent[i]\n\n    def union(i, j):\n        root_i = find(i)\n        root_j = find(j)\n        if root_i != root_j:\n            parent[root_i] = root_j\n\n    for _ in range(M):\n        u = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        v = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        union(u, v)\n\n    K = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    # Store restricted component pairs as a set of frozensets for O(1) lookup\n    restricted_components = set()\n    for _ in range(K):\n        x = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        y = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        root_x = find(x)\n        root_y = find(y)\n        # Store as sorted tuple to handle symmetry\n        if root_x > root_y:\n            root_x, root_y = root_y, root_x\n        restricted_components.add((root_x, root_y))\n\n    Q = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n    results = []\n    for _ in range(Q):\n        p = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        q = int(input[idx]); idx += 1\n        root_p = find(p)\n        root_q = find(q)\n        \n        if root_p > root_q:\n            root_p, root_q = root_q, root_p\n            \n        if (root_p, root_q) in restricted_components:\n            results.append(\"No\")\n        else:\n            results.append(\"Yes\")\n            \n    sys.stdout.write('\\n'.join(results) + '\\n')\n\nif __name__ == '__main__':\n    # Increase recursion depth for deep DSU trees\n    sys.setrecursionlimit(300000)\n    solve()\n```",
