      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we need to calculate the **expected number of green connected components** after repainting **one red cell (.)** to green (**#**), chosen uniformly at random.\n\nThe final answer is this expected value — a rational number — **modulo 998244353**, which means we need to express the answer as an integer $ R $ such that:\n\n$$\nR \\equiv \\frac{P}{Q} \\pmod{998244353}\n$$\n\nusing modular arithmetic (i.e., multiply numerator by modular inverse of denominator).\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Plan:\n\n1. **Input parsing**: Read the grid.\n2. **Count red cells** (i.e., `'.'`): let total_red = N.\n3. **Compute initial green connected components** (using BFS/DFS).\n4. **For each red cell**, simulate turning it green and:\n   - Compute how the number of green connected components changes.\n   - Instead of running full BFS for each case, we **analyze how many adjacent green components** the red cell connects.\n     - A red cell, when turned green, may **merge** several existing green components.\n     - The new number of components = old_components - (number of adjacent distinct components) + 1\n     - But wait: if the red cell connects k neighboring green components, then:\n       - Before: those were k separate components (or fewer, if some neighbors belong to same component)\n       - After: those k components may become 1 component (if k ≥ 1), plus the new cell.\n       - So **change in count**: $ \\text{old} - (k - 1) $ → new component count = $ \\text{old} - k + 1 $\n       - But actually: suppose the red cell has neighbors from `c` different components.\n         - Then, turning it green merges those `c` components into one, and adds 1 cell.\n         - So change: `new_components = old_components - c + 1`\n\n         Example:\n         - old = 5\n         - red cell touches 3 different components → merging them into one\n         - so we remove 2 components → new = 5 - 2 = 3\n         - or: 5 - 3 + 1 = 3\n\n     - Therefore:\n       $$\n       \\Delta = \\text{new\\_comps} = \\text{initial\\_comps} - c + 1\n       $$\n       where `c` = number of **distinct** green connected components adjacent to the red cell.\n\n5. Sum over all red cells: compute total expected value:\n   $$\n   E = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum_{\\text{each red cell}} (\\text{initial\\_comps} - c + 1)\n   $$\n\n6. But note: when we add a green cell, the initial number of components `init_comps` is not necessarily updated independently because the new cell might:\n   - Not touch any green → adds a new component → new = init_comps + 1\n   - Touch 1 green comp → merges into it → new = init_comps\n   - Touch 2 green comp → merges them → new = init_comps - 1\n   - Touch 3 → merges 3 → new = init_comps - 2\n   - Touch 4 → merges 4 → new = init_comps - 3\n\n   So: **new_comps = init_comps - (c - 1) = init_comps - c + 1**\n\n   This formula works:\n   - c = 0 → init_comps + 1\n   - c = 1 → init_comps → no change\n   - c = 2 → init_comps -1\n   - ...\n\n7. So we do:\n   - Precompute connected components of green cells, assign each green cell a `component_id`.\n   - For each red cell, look at neighbors, collect the set of component IDs (ignore if neighbor is not green).\n   - Let `c = len(set of component IDs from neighbors)`\n   - Then new component count for this repaint = `init_comps - c + 1`\n\n8. Total expected value:\n   $$\n   E = \\frac{1}{N} \\sum (\\text{init\\_comps} - c + 1)\n   = \\frac{1}{N} \\left( N \\cdot (\\text{init\\_comps} + 1) - \\sum c \\right)\n   = \\text{init\\_comps} + 1 - \\frac{\\sum c}{N}\n   $$\n\n   But easier: just sum over each red cell.\n\n9. Convert this rational number $ \\frac{P}{Q} $ to modulo 998244353:\n   - Use modular inverse of $ Q $ modulo 998244353.\n\n---\n\n### 🔢 Modular Arithmetic Helper:\n\nWe define:\n```python\nMOD = 998244353\n```\n\nTo compute modular inverse: use `pow(a, MOD-2, MOD)`\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Implementation Steps:\n\n1. Read input.\n2. Count red cells and store their positions.\n3. Build green graph and compute connected components (using BFS/DFS), assign each green cell its component id.\n4. For each red cell:\n   - Check neighbors (up, down, left, right).\n   - Collect component IDs of green neighbors.\n   - Let `c = len(set(ids))`\n   - Add `init_comps - c + 1` to total sum.\n5. Expected value = total_sum / N → modulo MOD.\n\n---\n\nLet’s write the full code accordingly.\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    if not data: \n        return\n    \n    H, W = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = []\n    for i in range(1, 1+H):\n        grid.append(data[i].strip())\n    \n    # Directions for neighbors\n    dx = [-1, 1, 0, 0]\n    dy = [0, 0, -1, 1]\n    \n    # Step 1: Find all green cells and compute connected components\n    visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    comp_id = [[-1] * W for _ in range(H)]  # -1 means no component (red or not visited)\n    comp_count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#' and not visited[i][j]:\n                # BFS\n                q = deque([(i, j)])\n                visited[i][j] = True\n                comp_id[i][j] = comp_count\n                while q:\n                    x, y = q.popleft()\n                    for d in range(4):\n                        nx, ny = x + dx[d], y + dy[d]\n                        if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W and not visited[nx][ny] and grid[nx][ny] == '#':\n                            visited[nx][ny] = True\n                            comp_id[nx][ny] = comp_count\n                            q.append((nx, ny))\n                comp_count += 1\n    \n    initial_components = comp_count\n    \n    # Step 2: Find all red cells\n    red_cells = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                red_cells.append((i, j))\n    \n    total_red = len(red_cells)\n    \n    # If no red cells, problem guarantees at least one, but still safe\n    if total_red == 0:\n        print(0)\n        return\n    \n    total_sum = 0  # Sum of resulting component counts over all red cell choices\n    \n    # For each red cell, simulate painting it green\n    for (i, j) in red_cells:\n        neighbor_components = set()\n        for d in range(4):\n            ni, nj = i + dx[d], j + dy[d]\n            if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '#':\n                neighbor_components.add(comp_id[ni][nj])\n        \n        c = len(neighbor_components)\n        new_components = initial_components - c + 1\n        total_sum += new_components\n    \n    # Expected value = total_sum / total_red mod 998244353\n    # total_sum and total_red are integers\n    # So: (total_sum * inv(total_red)) mod MOD\n    \n    inv_red = pow(total_red, MOD-2, MOD)\n    result = (total_sum % MOD) * inv_red % MOD\n    print(result)\n\nmain()\n```\n\n---\n\n### ✅ Explanation with Sample 1:\n\nGrid:\n```\n##.\n#.#\n#..\n```\n\nInitial green cells: all `#` → we have a connected component?\n\nLet’s see:\n- (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (2,0) → all connected? Yes: (0,0) → (0,1), (1,0) → (2,0)\n- (0,1) and (1,1) → no, (1,1) is red → so (0,0)-(0,1), (1,0), (2,0): are they connected?\n  - (0,0) down to (1,0) → yes\n  - (1,0) down to (2,0) → yes\n  - (0,1) has no green down/right? (1,1) is red\n  - So (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (2,0) all connected? \n    - (0,0) → (0,1) → right\n    - (0,0) → (1,0) → down\n    - (1,0) → (2,0) → down\n    - So one component.\n\n(1,1) is '.', (1,2) is '#': (1,2) is green?\n\nWait:\nRow 0: \"##.\" → [0,0]#, [0,1]#, [0,2].\nRow 1: \"#.#\" → [1,0]#, [1,1]., [1,2]#\nRow 2: \"#..\" → [2,0]#, [2,1]., [2,2].\n\nSo green cells: (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,2), (2,0)\n\nNow check components:\n\n- Component 1:\n  - (0,0): neighbors (0,1) and (1,0) → all green\n  - (0,1): down (1,1) is red → so not connected to (1,2)\n  - (1,0) → (2,0)\n  - So (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (2,0) connected → comp 0\n\n- (1,2): up=(0,2)=. → down=(2,2)=. → left=(1,1)=. → so isolated → comp 1\n\nSo initial_components = 2\n\nRed cells: (0,2), (1,1), (2,1), (2,2) → 4\n\nNow for each:\n\n1. (0,2): neighbors:\n   - (0,1): # → comp0\n   - (1,2): # → comp1\n   → c = 2\n   → new_comps = 2 - 2 + 1 = 1\n\n2. (1,1): neighbors:\n   - (0,1): comp0\n   - (1,0): comp0\n   - (1,2): comp1\n   - (2,1): .\n   → components: {0,1} → c=2 → new_comps = 1\n\n3. (2,1): neighbors:\n   - (1,1): .\n   - (2,0): comp0\n   - (2,2): .\n   - (3,1): invalid\n   → only (2,0): comp0 → c=1 → new_comps = 2 -1 +1 = 2\n\n4. (2,2): neighbors:\n   - (1,2): comp1\n   - (2,1): .\n   → c=1 → new_comps = 2\n\nTotal sum = 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 = 6\nExpected = 6/4 = 3/2\n\nNow compute $ 3/2 \\mod 998244353 $:\n\n- $ 3 \\times \\text{inv}(2) \\mod 998244453 $\n- $ \\text{inv}(2) = pow(2, MOD-2, MOD) $\n- $ 2^{-1} \\mod 998244353 = (998244353+1)//2 = 499122177 $ ? Wait: check: 2 * 499122177 = 998244354 ≡ 1 mod 998244353 → yes.\n\nSo $ 3 * 499122177 = 1497366531 \\mod 998244353 $\n\nBut wait: 3/2 = 3 * inv(2) → so 3 * 499122177 = 1497366531\n\nNow mod 998244353:\n\n1497366531 % 998244353 = ?\n\n998244353 * 1 = 998244353\n\n1497366531 - 998244353 = 499122178 → which matches sample output.\n\nSo output: **499122178**\n\n✅ Matches.\n\n---\n\nFinal code below:\n\n---\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import deque\n\nMOD = 998244353\n\ndef main():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().splitlines()\n    H, W = map(int, data[0].split())\n    grid = [line.strip() for line in data[1:1+H]]\n    \n    dx = [-1, 1, 0, 0]\n    dy = [0, 0, -1, 1]\n    \n    # Build component IDs for green cells\n    visited = [[False] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    comp_id = [[-1] * W for _ in range(H)]\n    comp_count = 0\n    \n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '#' and not visited[i][j]:\n                q = deque([(i, j]))\n                visited[i][j] = True\n                comp_id[i][j] = comp_count\n                while q:\n                    x, y = q.popleft()\n                    for d in range(4):\n                        nx, ny = x + dx[d], y + dy[d]\n                        if 0 <= nx < H and 0 <= ny < W and grid[nx][ny] == '#' and not visited[nx][ny]:\n                            visited[nx][ny] = True\n                            comp_id[nx][ny] = comp_count\n                            q.append((nx, ny))\n                comp_count += 1\n    \n    initial_components = comp_count\n    \n    # Get red cells\n    red_cells = []\n    for i in range(H):\n        for j in range(W):\n            if grid[i][j] == '.':\n                red_cells.append((i, j))\n    \n    total_red = len(red_cells)\n    total_sum = 0\n    \n    for (i, j) in red_cells:\n        neighbor_comps = set()\n        for d in range(4):\n            ni, nj = i + dx[d], j + dy[d]\n            if 0 <= ni < H and 0 <= nj < W and grid[ni][nj] == '#':\n                neighbor_comps.add(comp_id[ni][nj])\n        c = len(neighbor_comps)\n        new_comps = initial_components - c + 1\n        total_sum += new_comps\n    \n    # Expected value = total_sum / total_red mod MOD\n    inv_red = pow(total_red, MOD-2, MOD)\n    result = total_sum % MOD\n    result = (result * inv_red) % MOD\n    print(result)\n\nmain()\n```\n\nThis is the complete executable solution.",
