      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the sum of power for all non-empty groups of heroes, where the power of a group is (max^2) * min.\n\nThe key insight is that instead of generating all possible subsets (which would be 2^n and too slow), I can sort the array and use a more efficient approach by considering each element's contribution.\n\nWhen we sort the array, for each element at position i (0-indexed), I can calculate how many subsets have this element as the maximum, and what their minimum values are.\n\nFor a sorted array, if nums[i] is the maximum of a subset, then all other elements in the subset must come from indices 0 to i-1. For each possible minimum value nums[j] where j ≤ i, I need to count how many subsets have nums[i] as maximum and nums[j] as minimum.\n\nHowever, there's an even more efficient approach: for each element at position i (after sorting), I can calculate its contribution when it serves as the maximum element.\n\nHere's the optimized solution:\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    result = 0\n    sum_min = 0  # This will track the sum of min values for subsets ending at current position\n    \n    for i in range(len(nums)):\n        # For the current element nums[i], it will be the maximum\n        # Any subset with maximum nums[i] will contribute: nums[i]^2 * (sum of min values of all such subsets)\n        \n        # When nums[i] is added, it can form new subsets:\n        # 1. Single element subset [nums[i]] - min is nums[i]\n        # 2. Subsets ending with nums[i] where minimum comes from previous elements\n        \n        # The sum of min values for all subsets where nums[i] is maximum:\n        # = nums[i] (for the singleton) + sum_min (for subsets where we add nums[i] to existing subsets)\n        \n        total_min_sum = (nums[i] + sum_min) % MOD\n        \n        # Add contribution: nums[i]^2 * total_min_sum\n        contribution = ((nums[i] * nums