      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem efficiently, we sort the `nums` array. For each element `nums[i]`, we treat it as the maximum value in a group. To avoid overcounting or missing groups, we use the property that for a sorted array, the \"power\" of a group where `nums[i]` is the maximum and `nums[j]` (where $j \\le i$) is the minimum is defined by $nums[i]^2 \\times nums[j]$. \n\nWe use a monotonic stack to find the previous smaller element and the next smaller element to efficiently calculate the contribution of each element as the minimum, but here, since the maximum is fixed, we iterate through the sorted array and maintain a running sum of the potential minimums.\n\n```python\ndef sumOfPower(nums):\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # left[i] stores the index of the previous element smaller than nums[i]\n    left = [-1] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] >= nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            left[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n    \n    # right[i] stores the index of the next element smaller than or equal to nums[i]\n    right = [n] * n\n    stack = []\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        while stack and nums[stack[-1]] > nums[i]:\n            stack.pop()\n        if stack:\n            right[i] = stack[-1]\n        stack.append(i)\n        \n    # f[i] is the sum of minimums of all subarrays where nums[i] is the minimum\n    # f[i] = nums[i] * (i - left[i]) + f[left[i]] if left[i] != -1\n    f = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        l = left[i]\n        count = i - l\n        f[i] = (nums[i] * count + (f[l] if l != -1 else 0)) % MOD\n        \n    ans = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # When nums[i] is the maximum, the groups are formed by \n        # picking any subarray ending at i where nums[i] is max.\n        # This is equivalent to summing (nums[i]^2 * sum_of_mins_in_subarrays)\n        # Using the logic of contribution:\n        r = right[i]\n        count = r - i\n        contribution = (f[i] * nums[i]) % MOD\n        ans = (ans + contribution * nums[i]) % MOD\n        \n    return ans\n```",
