    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the number of ways to express `n` as the sum of unique positive integers raised to the power `x`.\n\nThis is a variation of the subset sum problem where:\n1. I need to find all possible positive integers whose x-th power is ≤ n\n2. I need to count the number of subsets of these numbers that sum exactly to n\n3. Each number can be used at most once (unique integers)\n\nI'll use dynamic programming where `dp[i]` represents the number of ways to achieve sum `i`.\n\nHere's my approach:\n1. Generate all possible x-th powers that are ≤ n\n2. Use DP to count the number of ways to achieve each sum\n3. For each power, update the DP array in reverse order to avoid using the same number multiple times\n\n```python\ndef numberOfWays(n, x):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # Generate all numbers whose x-th power is <= n\n    powers = []\n    i = 1\n    while i**x <= n:\n        powers.append(i**x)\n        i += 1\n    \n    # DP array where dp[i] = number of ways to make sum i\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1  # One way to make sum 0: use no numbers\n    \n    # For each power, update the dp array\n    for power in powers:\n        # Traverse backwards to avoid using same number multiple times\n        for j in range(n, power - 1, -1):\n            dp[j] = (dp[j] + dp[j - power]) % MOD\n    \n    return dp[n]\n```",
