      "generated_answer": "```python\ndef count_ways(n, x):\n    \"\"\"\n    To solve this, we use dynamic programming. We want to find the number of \n    ways to choose unique positive integers such that the sum of their x^th \n    powers equals n.\n    \n    Let dp[i] be the number of ways to form the sum i.\n    We iterate through all possible base integers i such that i^x <= n.\n    For each such i, we update the dp table. Since each integer can be used\n    only once, we update the dp table in reverse order to avoid using the same\n    element multiple times.\n    \"\"\"\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    \n    # dp[j] will store the number of ways to get sum j\n    dp = [0] * (n + 1)\n    dp[0] = 1\n    \n    # Generate all possible values i^x that are <= n\n    powers = []\n    i = 1\n    while True:\n        p = i ** x\n        if p > n:\n            break\n        powers.append(p)\n        i += 1\n        \n    # Update DP table for each power\n    for p in powers:\n        # Traverse backwards to ensure each power is used at most once\n        for j in range(n, p - 1, -1):\n            dp[j] = (dp[j] + dp[j - p]) % MOD\n            \n    return dp[n]\n```",
