array.\n\nWait — no: each original bit in position `b` appears in `cnt_b` elements. So we can **assign that bit to at most `cnt_b` of the final values** (but not more). Since the total number of times bit `b` can appear across all array elements is fixed.\n\nBut if we choose `k` elements to maximize sum of squares, we can simulate:\n- We are building `k` numbers.\n- For each bit `b`, we can assign it to **at most `min(cnt_b, k)` of these k numbers**, but actually, we can assign it to **up to `cnt_b`** numbers (even all k if `cnt_b >= k`), so we **can give bit `b` to up to `cnt_b` of the chosen values**.\n\nBut we want to **maximize sum of squares**, which is:\n> Σ (x_i)^2\n\nNow, distribute bits optimally over `k` numbers to maximize sum of squares.\n\nHowever, there's a known trick from similar LeetCode problems (e.g. “Maximize Sum of Squares After Operations”):\n\n> We can simulate the **maximum possible values** we can form using greedy bit allocation.\n\n### ✅ Correct Approach:\n\nLet’s consider that:\n- We can **rearrange bits freely** under the constraint that each bit can only be used as many times as it originally appeared across all elements (in total).\n\nSo:\n- We are to assign bits to at most `k` numbers (we pick `k` final numbers).\n- For each bit `b` (0 to 31), we have `cnt[b]` copies available.\n- We can assign a copy of bit `b` to any of the `k` numbers, at most `cnt[b]` times in total.\n\nOur goal: choose up to `k` numbers (exactly `k`) such that the **sum of squares is maximized**.\n\nThis is equivalent to building `k` integers where:\n- Each bit `b` is used in at most `cnt[b]` of them.\n\nWe want to maximize sum of squares.\n\nBut sum of squares is not linear, so we can't just assign high bits greedily to one number.\n\nHowever, note: **The square function is convex**, so it's better to **concentrate bits into fewer numbers**.\n\nBut we must pick **exactly k numbers**. So we need to form exactly `k` numbers (possibly with 0s), and maximize the sum