    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. The function should take stdin as input and print the output. Simply call the function after the definition. Question:\nThere is a family consisting of person 1, person 2, \\ldots, and person N.  For i\\geq 2, person i's parent is person p_i.\nThey bought insurance M times.  For i=1,2,\\ldots,M, person x_i bought the i-th insurance, which covers that person and their descendants in the next y_i generations.  \nHow many people are covered by at least one insurance?\n\nInput\n\nThe input is given from Standard Input in the following format:\nN M\r\np_2 \\ldots p_N\r\nx_1 y_1\r\n\\vdots\r\nx_M y_M\n\nOutput\n\nPrint the answer.\n\nConstraints\n\n\n- 2 \\leq N \\leq 3 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq M \\leq 3 \\times 10^5\n- 1 \\leq p_i \\leq i-1\n- 1 \\leq x_i \\leq N\n- 1 \\leq y_i \\leq 3 \\times 10^5\n- All input values are integers.\n\nSample Input 1\n\n7 3\r\n1 2 1 3 3 3\r\n1 1\r\n1 2\r\n4 3\n\nSample Output 1\n\n4\r\n\nThe 1-st insurance covers people 1, 2, and 4, because person 1's 1-st generation descendants are people 2 and 4.\r\nThe 2-nd insurance covers people 1, 2, 3, and 4, because person 1's 1-st generation descendants are people 2 and 4, and person 1's 2-nd generation descendant is person 3.\r\nThe 3-rd insurance covers person 4, because person 4 has no 1-st, 2-nd, or 3-rd descendants.  \nTherefore, four people, people 1, 2, 3, and 4, are covered by at least one insurance.\n\nSample Input 2\n\n10 10\r\n1 1 3 1 2 3 3 5 7\r\n2 1\r\n5 1\r\n4 3\r\n6 3\r\n2 1\r\n7 3\r\n9 2\r\n1 2\r\n6 2\r\n8 1\n\nSample Output 2\n\n10",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "I'll solve this step by step.\n\n## Problem Understanding\n\n1. We have a family tree where person i (for i≥2) has parent p_i\n2. Each insurance policy i covers person x_i and their descendants up to y_i generations\n3. We need to count how many distinct people are covered by at least one insurance\n\n## Key Insights\n\n1. We need to build the family tree from parent relationships\n2. For each insurance, we need to find all descendants within y_i generations of x_i\n3. A naive approach (for each insurance, BFS/DFS up to y_i levels) might be too slow for constraints up to 3×10^5\n\n## Optimized Approach\n\nInstead of directly finding covered people, we can use a \"reverse\" approach:\n- For each person, find the maximum coverage depth that reaches them\n- A person is covered if there exists an insurance (x, y) such that x is an ancestor of the person (including self) and the distance from x to person ≤ y\n\nLet depth[v] be the depth of person v (depth of root=0)\nLet dist(x, v) be the distance from x to v (if x is ancestor of v) = depth[v] - depth[x]\n\nPerson v is covered if there exists insurance (x, y) such that:\n- x is ancestor of v (including v itself)\n- depth[v] - depth[x] ≤ y\n- i.e., depth[v] ≤ depth[x] + y\n\nSo for each person v, we need to check if max_{x is ancestor of v} (depth[x] + y_x) ≥ depth[v], where y_x is the maximum y value among insurances bought by person x.\n\nActually, we can process this more efficiently using DFS and maintaining the maximum \"coverage threshold\" along the path.\n\n## Algorithm\n\n1. Build the tree and compute depths\n2. For each person, store the maximum y value among insurances they bought\n3. Do DFS from root, maintaining max_coverage = max over ancestors of (depth[ancestor] + max_y[ancestor])\n4. A person is covered if max_coverage >= depth[person]\n\nLet me implement this:\n\n```python\nimport sys\nfrom collections import defaultdict, deque\n\ndef solve():\n    data = sys.stdin.read().strip().split()\n    if not data:\n        return\n    \n    it = iter(data)\n    N = int(next(it))\n    M = int(next(it))\n    \n    # Build parent array and children adjacency list\n    parent = [0] * (N + 1)\n    children = [[] for _ in range(N + 1)]\n    \n    # For i from 2 to N, read parent p_i\n    for i in range(2, N + 1):\n        p = int(next(it))\n        parent[i] = p\n        children[p].append(i)\n    \n    # max_y[i] = max y among insurances bought by person i\n    max_y = [0] * (N + 1)\n    \n    # Read M insurances\n    for _ in range(M):\n        x = int(next(it))\n        y = int(next(it))\n        max_y[x] = max(max_y[x], y)\n    \n    # Compute depths using BFS from root (person 1)\n    depth = [-1] * (N + 1)\n    depth[1] = 0\n    queue = deque([1])\n    \n    while queue:\n        node = queue.popleft()\n        for child in children[node]:\n            depth[child] = depth[node] + 1\n            queue.append(child)\n    \n    # DFS to check coverage\n    # For each node, track max(depth[ancestor] + max_y[ancestor]) along the path\n    covered_count = 0\n    \n    def dfs(node, max_cover):\n        nonlocal covered_count\n        \n        # max_cover is max over ancestors (including current) of (depth[ancestor] + max_y[ancestor])\n        max_cover = max(max_cover, depth[node] + max_y[node])\n        \n        # Check if current node is covered\n        if max_cover >= depth[node]:\n            covered_count += 1\n        \n        # Process children\n        for child in children[node]:\n            dfs(child, max_cover)\n    \n    dfs(1, 0)\n    print(covered_count)\n\nsolve()\n```",
