 # But 50 choose 25 is about 1.26e14, which is too large.\n    #\n    # Let's use a different DP approach:\n    #\n    # dp[i][l][prev] = (count, min_diff_sum) for subsequences of length l ending at index i,\n    # where the previous selected element was at index prev (prev < i), and we track the minimum\n    # difference seen so far in the subsequence. But tracking min_diff as a state is problematic\n    # because min_diff can be large and we need to sum contributions.\n    #\n    # Better idea: Instead of tracking the minimum difference directly, we can use the fact that\n    # the minimum difference in a subsequence is equal to the minimum difference between any two\n    # consecutive elements in the sorted subsequence. So if we sort nums first and consider only\n    # subsequences (which when sorted have elements in increasing order), the minimum difference\n    # is the minimum of (nums[idx[k]] - nums[idx[k-1]]) for consecutive indices in the subsequence.\n    #\n    # We can use DP where:\n    # dp[i][l] = a list of (min_diff, count) for all subsequences of length l ending at index i.\n    # But the number of distinct min_diff values could be large.\n    #\n    # Another idea: Since n is small (<= 50), we can iterate over all possible minimum differences\n    # and count how many subsequences have that exact minimum difference.\n    #\n    # Let's denote by D the set of all possible absolute differences: D = {abs(nums[i] - nums[j]) : i < j}\n    # There are at most n*(n-1)/2 = 1225 distinct differences.\n    #\n    # For a given d, let f(d) be the number of k-length subsequences with power exactly d.\n    # Then the answer is sum_{d} d * f(d).\n    #\n    # How to compute f(d)? \n    # A subsequence has power >= d if all consecutive differences in the sorted subsequence are >= d.\n    # A subsequence has power > d if all consecutive differences in the sorted subsequence are > d.\n    # So f(d) = g(>=d) - g(>d), where g(>=d) is the number of k-length subsequences with all consecutive differences >= d.\n    #\n    # But note: power = minimum difference, so power >= d means min diff >= d, and power > d means min diff > d.\n    # So f(d) = count(power >= d) - count(power >= d+1) ??? Not exactly, because d might not be integer step.\n    # Actually, since differences are integers (because nums[i] are integers), d is integer.\n    # So f(d) = count(power >= d) - count(power >= d+1).\n    #\n    # Then answer = sum_d d * (count(power >= d) - count(power >= d+1))\n    #            = sum_d count(power >= d)   [by telescoping sum]\n    #\n    # Let's verify: \n    # Let F(d) = count(power >= d). Then answer = sum_{d=1}^{max_d} F(d).\n    # Because:\n    #   sum_{d} d * (F(d) - F(d+1)) = sum_{d} F(d) * (d - (d-1)) ??? Let's compute:\n    #   = 1*(F(1)-F(2)) + 2*(F(2)-F(3)) + 3*(F(3)-F(4)) + ... \n    #   = F(1) + F(2) + F(3) + ... \n    # Yes! So answer = sum_{d=1}^{max_d} F(d), where F(d) = number of k-length subsequences with min diff >= d.\n    #\n    # Now, how to compute F(d) for a given d?\n    # F(d) = number of k-length subsequences where all consecutive differences in the sorted subsequence are >= d.\n    # Since we sorted nums, we can use DP:\n    # Let dp[i][l] = number of ways to form a subsequence of length l ending at index i, such that all consecutive differences are >= d.\n    # Transition: dp[i][l] = sum_{j < i and nums[i] - nums[j] >= d} dp[j][l-1]\n    # Base: dp[i][1] = 1 for all i.\n    # Then F(d) = sum_{i} dp[i][k].\n    #\n    # The maximum possible d is max(nums) - min(nums), which is <= 2e8, but we only need to iterate over d that appear as differences or are relevant.\n    # But note: we only need F(d) for d that are differences or where F(d) changes. Actually, F(d) is a step function that changes only at values in D.\n    # Since D has at most O(n^2) = 1225 values, we can iterate over all d in D and compute F(d).\n    #\n    # However, note that d must be positive integer, and we need to consider all d from 1 to max_d, but max_d can be 2e8, which is too big.\n    # Instead, we can iterate over the distinct differences and use the fact that F(d) is constant between consecutive distinct difference values.\n    #\n    # Let D_sorted = sorted(set(abs(nums[i]-nums[j]) for i<j))  # but we need differences, and since sorted, just nums[j]-nums[i] for j>i\n    # Actually, since nums is sorted, differences are nums[j]-nums[i] for j>i, all non-negative.\n    # Let D = sorted(set(nums[j]-nums[i] for i in range(n) for j in range(i+1,n)))  # list of distinct positive differences\n    # But we also need d=1,2,... up to max_diff, but note that F(d) only changes at values in D and 0.\n    # Actually, F(d) = F(floor(d)) for real d, and since differences are integers, F(d) is constant on intervals [d_i, d_{i+1}), so we can compute:\n    # answer = sum_{i} F(d_i) * (d_{i+1} - d_i) ??? No, because our earlier derivation was for integer d and answer = sum_{d=1}^{max_d} F(d).\n    # But note: F(d) is defined for integer d, and for d not in the range of differences, F(d) is the same as F(d') where d' is the next smaller difference that exists.\n    # Actually, F(d) is non-increasing and stepwise constant. Let the distinct values of d where F(d) changes be: 1 = d0 < d1 < ... < dm.\n    # But actually, F(d) = F(d') for all d in [d_i, d_{i+1}), so for integer d, F(d) is constant on intervals of integers.\n    # However, since differences are integers, F(d) changes only at integer values that are in the set of differences? Not exactly: \n    # F(d) = number of subsequences with min diff >= d. So if d1 < d2, then F(d1) >= F(d2). And F(d) changes only when d passes a value that is the min diff of some subsequence.\n    # But the min diff of a subsequence is one of the differences (nums[j]-nums[i] for some i<j in the subsequence). So the critical d values are the differences that appear as min diff of some subsequence, but also any integer between 1 and max_diff.\n    # However, we don't need to