ce of two elements, so at most O(n^2) distinct values, and n<=50, so at most 1225 per state.\n    # Total states: n * k = 50 * 50 = 2500, so total entries 2500 * 1225 = ~3e6, which is acceptable in Python if implemented carefully (but worst-case might be borderline in Pyton in worst contest, but n<=50 and k<=50, and 50*50*1225=3,062,500 which is acceptable in Pyton with pypy or in C++ but in Python it might be a bit slow but 3e6 is acceptable in Pyton for online judges usually).\n    #\n    # Steps for this DP:\n    #   Sort nums.\n    #   dp[i][l] = defaultdict(int)  # dp[i][l][min_diff] = count of subsequences of length l ending at i with min_diff = min_diff\n    #   Base: for all i, dp[i][1] = { infinity: 1 } but actually for length 1, there is no pair, so min_diff is undefined. But we can say for length 1, we don't have a power, but we only care about length k>=2.\n    #   However, the problem: subsequences of length k, and k>=2.\n    #   So we can start from l=2.\n    #   For l=2: for each pair (i, j) with i<j, dp[j][2][nums[j]-nums[i]] = 1.\n    #   For l>2: to form a subsequence ending at j of length l, we take a subsequence ending at i (i<j) of length l-1, and add j. The new min_diff = min(old_min_diff, nums[j]-nums[i]).\n    #   So:\n    #       for each i < j:\n    #          diff = nums[j] - nums[i]\n    #          for each (old_min, cnt) in dp[i][l-1]:\n    #               new_min = min(old_min, diff)\n    #               dp[j][l][new_min] += cnt\n    #   Then answer = sum_{j} sum_{(min_diff, cnt) in dp[j][k]} min_diff * cnt\n    #\n    # But note: the state for l=1: we can consider for l=1, we store a dummy min_diff = infinity (so that when we add the second element, new_min = min(inf, diff)=diff).\n    # So:\n    #   dp[i][1] = { float('inf'): 1 } for all i.\n    #   Then for l=2 to k:\n    #       for j from 0 to n-1:\n    #          for i from 0 to j-1:\n    #             diff = nums[j] - nums[i]\n    #             for (prev_min, cnt) in dp[i][l-1]:\n    #                 new_min = min(prev_min, diff)\n    #                 dp[j][l][new_min] += cnt\n    #\n    # However, the state size: for each (i, l), the number of keys in the dict is at most the number of distinct min_diff values that can occur ending at i for length l.\n    # But note: the min_diff for a subsequence ending at i is always one of the differences between two elements in the subsequence, and since the subsequence has l elements, there are l-1 consecutive differences, and the min_diff is the minimum of these. \n    # The distinct min_diff values are bounded by the number of distinct differences, which is O(n^2). So worst-case, each state has O(n^2) entries, and total states = n * k, so total operations = n * k * n * (number of states from previous) * (average state size) \n    # Actually, for each j and l, we iterate over i from 0 to j-1 (j choices), and for each i, we iterate over the state of dp[i][l-1] (which has at most O(n^2) entries). So total time = O(n^3 * k * n^2) = O(n^5 * k) = 50^5 * 50 = 50^6 = 15,625,000,000 which is too big.\n    #\n    # We need a better approach.\n    #\n    # Let me check known solutions for similar problems (like Leetcode 3018). \n    # Actually, this problem is similar to \"Sum of Powers of All Subsequences of Size K\" from a contest.\n    #\n    # Insight: The power (min absolute difference) of a subsequence is determined by the two elements that are closest together. So for each pair (i, j) (i<j), we can count the number of k-length subsequences that include both i and j and for which (i,j) is the pair with minimum difference (i.e., no other pair has smaller difference).\n    #\n    # Steps for this:\n    #   Sort nums.\n    #   For a fixed pair (i, j) (i<j), let d = nums[j]-nums[i].\n    #   We want to count the number of k-length subsequences that:\n    #       - include i and j\n    #       - all other elements are in the range [i, j] (because if we include an element outside [i,j], then the difference with i or j might be larger, but actually elements outside might create a smaller difference? No, because the array is sorted, so elements < nums[i] or > nums[j] would form larger differences with i and j, but might form a smaller difference with each other? However, if we include an element k < i, then the difference between k and i is nums[i]-nums[k], which might be < d. So to ensure that the minimum difference is exactly d, we require that there is no pair in the subsequence with difference < d.\n    #   So conditions:\n    #       1. The subsequence contains i and j.\n    #       2. All elements in the subsequence are in the set S = {x: nums[x] in [nums[i], nums[j]]} and moreover, for any two elements in the subsequence, their difference is at least d.\n    #          But note: the difference between i and j is d, and we want d to be the minimum, so all other pairs must have difference >= d.\n    #\n    #   However, it's complex to ensure that no pair has difference < d.\n    #\n    #   Alternative: for the pair (i,j) to be the pair with minimum difference, we require:\n    #       - All elements in the subsequence are in the interval [nums[i], nums[j]].\n    #       - There is no pair (p,q) in the subsequence such that nums[q]-nums[p] < d.\n    #\n    #   But note: if there is a pair (p,q) with difference < d, then that pair would be the minimum, not (i,j). So we require that in the subsequence, the minimum difference is exactly d, which means:\n    #       - The subsequence contains i and j.\n    #       - There is no pair with difference < d.\n    #       - And d is present (which it is, by i and j).\n    #\n    #   So the count for pair (i,j) is the number of ways to choose k-2 elements from the set of indices between i and j (inclusive) such that:\n    #       - The chosen elements (along with i and j) form a set where the minimum difference is at least d, and since we have i and j with difference d, the minimum will be <= d, and if we ensure no difference < d, then minimum = d.\n    #   But wait, if we ensure that all consecutive differences in the sorted subsequence are >= d, then the minimum difference is >= d, and since we have a pair with difference d, the minimum is exactly d.\n    #\n    #   So for fixed (i,j), let L = i, R = j.\n    #   We consider only indices from i to j (inclusive). Let A = nums[i:j+1].\n    #   We want to count the number of subsets of A of size k that include i and j and such that for every consecutive pair in the sorted subset (which is just the subset sorted, and since A is sorted, it's the natural order), the difference is >= d.\n    #   But note: d = nums[j]-nums[i], and in the subarray A, the difference between the first and last is d, and we require all consecutive differences >= d. However, the consecutive difference between i and the next element must be >= d, but the next element is at least nums[i+1] >= nums[i] (and since sorted, nums[i+1] >= nums[i]), but nums[i+1]-nums[i] might be < d. In fact, it usually is.\n    #\n    #   This approach seems messy.\n    #\n    # Let me look for a known solution pattern.\n    #\n    # After checking, a known solution for this problem (Leetcode weekly contest) uses the following:\n    #   Sort the array.\n    #   Let dp[l][i] = the number of ways to choose a subsequence of length l ending at i, and also store the minimum difference in a smarter way.\n    #   But instead, we can use: \n    #       dp[i][l] = a list of minimum differences for subsequences ending at i of length l, but we can aggregate by the minimum difference.\n    #\n    # Given the constraints (n<=50), an O(n^3 * k) solution might be acceptable if we do it carefully.\n    # Let's try to optimize the DP:\n    #   We'll use dp[i][l] = a dictionary: min_diff -> count, for subsequences of length l ending at i.\n    #   But to avoid duplicate work, we can iterate in increasing order of i.\n    #   For l=1: dp[i][1] = { a big number: 1 } for all i. But since we only care about min_diff, and for l=1 there is no diff, we can set min_diff = 10**18 (a big number).\n    #   For l>=2:\n    #       for j in range(n):\n    #         for i in range(j):\n    #            diff = nums[j] - nums[i]\n    #            for prev_min, cnt in dp[i][l-1].items():\n    #                new_min = min(prev_min, diff)\n    #                dp[j][l][new_min] = (dp[j][l].get(new_min,0) + cnt) % MOD\n    #\n    #   Then answer = 0\n    #        for j in range(n):\n    #            for min_diff, cnt in dp[j][k].items():\n    #                answer = (answer + min_diff * cnt) % MOD\n    #\n    #   But time complexity: \n    #       for l from 2 to k: O(k)\n    #         for j from 0 to n-1: O(n)\n    #           for i from 0 to j-1: O(n)\n    #             for each state in dp[i][l-1]: O(number of states in dp[i][l-1])\n    #       Total: O(k * n^2 * S) where S is the average number of states per dp[i][l-1].\n    #   What is S? In the worst case, the number of distinct min_diff values for a fixed (i, l) is at most the number of distinct differences that can be the minimum in a subsequence ending at i of length l. \n    #   However, note that the min_diff is always one of the differences between two elements, and there are at most O(n^2) distinct differences. So S <= O(n^2).\n    #   So total time: O(k * n^2 * n^2) = O(k * n^4) = 50 * 50^4 = 50^5 = 312,500,000. \n    #   In Python, 300 million iterations might be borderline in speed (in C++ it's fine, in Pyton it might be 10-30 seconds), but the problem says n<=50, and worst-case 50^5=312.5e6 might be too slow in Python.\n    #\n    # We need to optimize.\n    #\n    # Optimization: \n    #   Notice that for a fixed ending index i and length l, the min_diff can only be one of the differences that are present in the array up to i. But more importantly, we can try to reduce the state.\n    #\n    #   Alternative approach: \n    #       Instead of storing the min_diff, store the last element and the previous element, because the min_diff is the minimum of the consecutive differences, and the consecutive differences are between consecutive chosen elements. \n    #       When we add a new element j after i, the new consecutive difference is nums[j]-nums[i], and the new min_diff = min(old_min_diff, nums[j]-nums[i]).\n    #       But the old_min_diff is the minimum of the consecutive differences in the subsequence ending at i, which includes the difference between the last two of that subsequence (say between i and some k) and possibly smaller ones.\n    #\n    #   However, we cannot avoid storing the min_diff because it is the minimum over all consecutive differences.\n    #\n    #   But note: the min_diff for a subsequence ending at i is determined by the smallest gap between any two consecutive elements in that subsequence. And when we extend to j, the only new consecutive difference is between i and j, so the new min_diff = min(old_min_diff, nums[j]-nums[i]).\n    #   So the state must include the old_min_diff.\n    #\n    #   Given the constraints (n=50, k=50), and that the number of distinct min_diff values per state is at most the number of distinct differences in the prefix, which is at most i*(i-1)/2 for index i, so worst-case for i=49, about 1225 states per state, and total states = n * k * (number of states per state) = 50 * 50 * 1225 = 3,062,500 states, and for each state, we do a loop over i (which is up to 50) for the previous ending index, so total operations = 3,062,500 * 50 = 153,125,000, which is acceptable in Pyton in PyPy or in C++ but in Python it might be borderline in speed (150 million iterations in Pyton might take 10-15 seconds in worst-case, but online judges might give 2-3 seconds for Pyton).\n    #\n    #   However, the problem says n<=50, and worst-case might be rare. Also, in practice, the number of distinct min_diff values might be less.\n    #\n    #   Let's try to implement this DP and hope that 150 million is acceptable in the online judge for Pyton. If no