    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given an integer array nums of length n, and a positive integer k.\nThe power of a subsequence is defined as the minimum absolute difference between any two elements in the subsequence.\nReturn the sum of powers of all subsequences of nums which have length equal to k.\nSince the answer may be large, return it modulo 10^9 + 7.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,3,4], k = 3\nOutput: 4\nExplanation:\nThere are 4 subsequences in nums which have length 3: [1,2,3], [1,3,4], [1,2,4], and [2,3,4]. The sum of powers is |2 - 3| + |3 - 4| + |2 - 1| + |3 - 4| = 4.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [2,2], k = 2\nOutput: 0\nExplanation:\nThe only subsequence in nums which has length 2 is [2,2]. The sum of powers is |2 - 2| = 0.\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput: nums = [4,3,-1], k = 2\nOutput: 10\nExplanation:\nThere are 3 subsequences in nums which have length 2: [4,3], [4,-1], and [3,-1]. The sum of powers is |4 - 3| + |4 - (-1)| + |3 - (-1)| = 10.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n2 <= n == nums.length <= 50\n-10^8 <= nums[i] <= 10^8 \n2 <= k <= n",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem efficiently given the constraints ($n \\le 50$), we first sort the array. By sorting, the \"power\" of a subsequence becomes the minimum difference between *adjacent* elements in the sorted subsequence. \n\nInstead of iterating through all $\\binom{n}{k}$ subsequences, we can use dynamic programming. Let $dp[i][j][last\\_idx][min\\_diff]$ be the number of subsequences of length $j$ ending at index $i$ with a minimum difference $min\\_diff$. However, since we only need the sum of minimum differences, we can reframe the problem: The sum of minimum differences is equal to $\\sum_{x} x \\cdot (\\text{count of subsequences with power } \\ge x)$. \n\nGiven the constraints and the nature of the \"minimum\" value, we can use DP: $dp[i][count][last\\_val]$ where we track the number of subsequences of length $count$ ending at index $i$ with a specific minimum difference.\n\n```python\ndef solve(nums, k):\n    MOD = 10**9 + 7\n    n = len(nums)\n    nums.sort()\n    \n    # dp[i][len][last_idx] stores the sum of powers of subsequences of length 'len'\n    # ending at 'last_idx'. However, since we need the minimum difference,\n    # we define dp[i][j][last_idx] as the sum of powers for subsequences of length j \n    # ending at i, with the last element being nums[i].\n    # This is tricky because the \"minimum\" is determined by all pairs.\n    \n    # Correct approach: Sort nums. A subsequence has power >= x if all adjacent \n    # elements in the sorted subsequence have difference >= x.\n    \n    def count_subsequences_with_min_diff_at_least(x):\n        # dp[i][len] = number of subsequences of length 'len' ending at index i\n        # such that all adjacent elements have diff >= x\n        dp = [[0] * (k + 1) for _ in range(n)]\n        for i in range(n):\n            dp[i][1] = 1\n            for j in range(i):\n                if nums[i] - nums[j] >= x:\n                    for l in range(2, k + 1):\n                        dp[i][l] = (dp[i][l] + dp[j][l-1]) % MOD\n        return sum(dp[i][k] for i in range(n)) % MOD\n\n    # The possible values of power are the differences between all pairs\n    diffs = sorted(list(set(nums[j] - nums[i] for i in range(n) for j in range(i + 1, n))))\n    \n    total_sum = 0\n    # Sum of powers = sum_{x in diffs} x * (count(power >= x) - count(power >= x_next))\n    prev_count = 0\n    for i in range(len(diffs) - 1, -1, -1):\n        x = diffs[i]\n        current_count = count_subsequences_with_min_diff_at_least(x)\n        total_sum = (total_sum + x * (current_count - prev_count)) % MOD\n        prev_count = current_count\n        \n    return total_sum\n```",
