    "prompt": "Generate an executable Python function generated from the given prompt. Return the function body without invoking it at the final solution. Question:\nYou are given a 0-indexed integer array nums and an integer k.\nYou can perform the following operation on the array at most k times:\n\nChoose any index i from the array and increase or decrease nums[i] by 1.\n\nThe score of the final array is the frequency of the most frequent element in the array.\nReturn the maximum score you can achieve.\nThe frequency of an element is the number of occurences of that element in the array.\n \nExample 1:\n\nInput: nums = [1,2,6,4], k = 3\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We can do the following operations on the array:\n- Choose i = 0, and increase the value of nums[0] by 1. The resulting array is [2,2,6,4].\n- Choose i = 3, and decrease the value of nums[3] by 1. The resulting array is [2,2,6,3].\n- Choose i = 3, and decrease the value of nums[3] by 1. The resulting array is [2,2,6,2].\nThe element 2 is the most frequent in the final array so our score is 3.\nIt can be shown that we cannot achieve a better score.\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput: nums = [1,4,4,2,4], k = 0\nOutput: 3\nExplanation: We cannot apply any operations so our score will be the frequency of the most frequent element in the original array, which is 3.\n\n \nConstraints:\n\n1 <= nums.length <= 10^5\n1 <= nums[i] <= 10^9\n0 <= k <= 10^14",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum frequency I can achieve by performing at most k operations where each operation increases or decreases an element by 1.\n\nThe key insight is that to maximize the frequency of a particular value, I should try to make as many elements as possible equal to that value. For any target value, I need to calculate the minimum operations needed to make x elements equal to that target.\n\nSince we want to maximize frequency, I can use binary search on the answer. For each possible frequency f, I need to check if it's possible to make f elements equal using at most k operations.\n\nFor a fixed frequency f, I can use a sliding window approach on the sorted array. For each window of size f, I need to find the minimum operations to make all elements in the window equal to some target value. The optimal target is the median of the window, as it minimizes the sum of absolute differences.\n\nHowever, since we can choose any target value (not necessarily one of the existing values), the optimal strategy is to pick a target and see how many elements we can convert to it within k operations.\n\nA better approach is to sort the array and use binary search on the maximum frequency. For each candidate frequency, I can use a sliding window and calculate the minimum operations needed to make all elements in the window equal.\n\nThe minimum operations to make all elements in a window equal to a target value is minimized when the target is the median. For a sorted window, the operations needed can be calculated efficiently.\n\n```python\ndef maxFrequencyScore(nums, k):\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    \n    def canAchieve(frequency):\n        if frequency > n:\n            return False\n        if frequency == 1:\n            return True\n            \n        # For each window of size frequency, check if we can make all elements equal\n        for i in range(n - frequency + 1):\n            window = nums[i:i + frequency]\n            # Find optimal target value - it should be one of the values in the original window\n            # The minimum operations is achieved when target is median or around median\n            min_ops = float('inf')\n            \n            # Try each possible target value in the range of the window\n            # We only need to check values that could be optimal\n            # The optimal target will be one of the existing values or constrained by the operation limit\n            left, right = window[0], window[-1]\n            \n            # For small windows, we can check all possible targets in the range\n            # But for efficiency, we can use the fact that optimal target is near the median\n            # Let's try the median and nearby values\n            median_idx = frequency // 2\n            candidates = []\n            candidates.append(window[median_idx])\n            if median_idx > 0:\n                candidates.append(window[median_idx - 1])\n            if median_idx < frequency - 1:\n                candidates.append(window[median_idx + 1])\n            \n            for target in set(candidates):\n                ops = sum(abs(x - target) for x in window)\n                min_ops = min(min_ops, ops)\n                \n                # Early termination if we find a valid target\n                if min_ops <= k:\n                    break\n            \n            if min_ops <= k:\n                return True\n                \n        return False\n    \n    # Binary search on the maximum frequency\n    left, right = 1, n\n    result = 1\n    \n    while left <= right:\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        if canAchieve(mid):\n            result = mid\n            left = mid + 1\n        else:\n            right = mid - 1\n    \n    return result\n```",
