      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem efficiently, we sort the array and use a sliding window approach. By treating a subarray `nums[i:j]` as a candidate to be made equal to the median (or any value within the range), we minimize the cost to make all elements in that window equal. The cost to make all elements in a window equal to the element at the median index is calculated using prefix sums.\n\n```python\ndef maxFrequency(nums, k):\n    nums.sort()\n    n = len(nums)\n    prefix_sums = [0] * (n + 1)\n    for i in range(n):\n        prefix_sums[i + 1] = prefix_sums[i] + nums[i]\n    \n    def get_cost(left, right):\n        # The optimal target value to minimize operations is the median element\n        mid = (left + right) // 2\n        median = nums[mid]\n        \n        # Elements to the left of median: (median * count) - sum\n        left_count = mid - left\n        left_sum = prefix_sums[mid] - prefix_sums[left]\n        cost_left = (left_count * median) - left_sum\n        \n        # Elements to the right of median: sum - (median * count)\n        right_count = right - mid\n        right_sum = prefix_sums[right + 1] - prefix_sums[mid + 1]\n        cost_right = right_sum - (right_count * median)\n        \n        return cost_left + cost_right\n\n    max_freq = 0\n    left = 0\n    for right in range(n):\n        while get_cost(left, right) > k:\n            left += 1\n        max_freq = max(max_freq, right - left + 1)\n        \n    return max_freq\n```",
