      "generated_answer": "To solve this problem, we can treat the indices as nodes in a graph. An edge exists between indices $i$ and $j$ if $\\gcd(nums[i], nums[j]) > 1$. However, with up to $10^5$ indices, we cannot build the graph explicitly. Instead, we use a Union-Find data structure to connect indices based on their prime factors. For each number in the array, we find its prime factors; every index containing a specific prime factor can be unified into the same component. If all indices belong to the same component, the function returns `true`.\n\n```python\ndef canTraverseAllPairs(nums: list[int]) -> bool:\n    n = len(nums)\n    if n == 1:\n        return True\n    if 1 in nums:\n        return False\n    \n    max_num = max(nums)\n    parent = list(range(n + max_num + 1))\n    \n    def find(i):\n        if parent[i] == i:\n            return i\n        parent[i] = find(parent[i])\n        return parent[i]\n    \n    def union(i, j):\n        root_i = find(i)\n        root_j = find(j)\n        if root_i != root_j:\n            parent[root_i] = root_j\n            \n    # Sieve to find smallest prime factor for factorization\n    spf = list(range(max_num + 1))\n    for i in range(2, int(max_num**0.5) + 1):\n        if spf[i] == i:\n            for j in range(i*i, max_num + 1, i):\n                if spf[j] == j:\n                    spf[j] = i\n                    \n    for i, num in enumerate(nums):\n        temp = num\n        while temp > 1:\n            p = spf[temp]\n            # Connect index i to the prime factor node (n + p)\n            union(i, n + p)\n            while temp % p == 0:\n                temp //= p\n                \n    # Check if all indices 0 to n-1 share the same root\n    root = find(0)\n    for i in range(1, n):\n        if find(i) != root:\n            return False\n            \n    return True\n```",
