    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Foot’s Rescue I and Rescue II cases are classic thought experiments in moral philosophy designed to explore the moral significance of action versus omission—specifically, whether actively causing harm is morally worse than passively allowing the same harm to occur. In Rescue I, one actively diverts a trolley to kill one person to save five; in Rescue II, one allows a trolley to continue and kill five when it could have been stopped with minimal effort. These cases primarily aim to contrast active harm with passive harm (Option C), which also inherently involves contrasting the origination of a harmful sequence (action) with allowing it to continue (omission) (Option A). However, since Option A is a more specific framing of the same distinction and Option C is the standard philosophical characterization, and given that B (\"all of the above\") is likely too broad, the best answer is **C**.\n\n\\boxed{C}",
