ed.  \nFinal check:  \n- A (~L): not derivable (needs *I* and *I ⊃ ~L* isn’t directly usable with the other premises).  \n- B (J ≡ K): same issue — needs *I*.  \n- D (~L ⊃ (J ≡ K)): implies that if ~L, then J ≡ K — but the premises only say (J ≡ K) → (L ∨ M), so if ~L, then ~M would be needed to get ~(L ∨ M), hence ~(J ≡ K) via Modus Tollens — so ~L does *not* imply (J ≡ K); in fact, under ~L and assuming M false, you’d get ~(J ≡ K). So D is false in some models of the premises — thus **not** a logical consequence at all, let alone immediate.  \nHence, **D** is not an immediate (or even mediate) consequence.  \nAnswer: $\\boxed{D}$",
