      "generated_answer": "The thinker referenced is **David Hume**, who argued in *A Treatise of Human Nature* that moral obligations persist even after the decline of divine-command theory, grounding them instead in human psychology and social sentiment. His analysis shows that the feeling of obligation endures not because it is divinely ordained, but due to its utility and emotional resonance in human interactions. While other philosophers like Kant later offered non-theistic accounts of obligation, Hume is most commonly associated with this specific insight about its survival post-divine framework.\n\n\\boxed{Hume}",
