    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The cost-minimizing dimensions for a cylinder of fixed volume $V = \\pi r^2 h = 10$ with top/bottom cost $2/\\text{ft}^2$ and side cost $1.5/\\text{ft}^2$ satisfy the condition $\\frac{dh}{dr} = -\\frac{2h}{r}$ from the volume constraint, and setting derivative of total cost $C = 4\\pi r^2 + 3\\pi r h$ (since top+bottom area = $2\\pi r^2$, side area = $2\\pi r h$) to zero yields $r = \\left(\\frac{3V}{4\\pi}\\right)^{1/3} \\approx 1.061$ ft and $h = \\frac{10}{\\pi r^2} \\approx 2.828$ ft. This matches option **A**.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
