    "global index": "MMLUPro_economics_5951",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: What is the difference between explicit and tacit collusion?\n\nOptions: \nA. Explicit collusion is an open agreement between companies on pricing, while tacit collusion implies similar pricing without any agreement.\nB. Explicit collusion involves a formal contract, while tacit collusion is based on informal social interactions.\nC. Explicit collusion is legal while tacit collusion is not\nD. Explicit collusion happens only in international markets, while tacit collusion is confined to domestic markets.\nE. Explicit collusion is based on verbal agreements, while tacit collusion relies on written contracts.\nF. Explicit collusion involves price fixing, while tacit collusion involves product quality fixation\nG. Explicit collusion occurs in retail markets, while tacit collusion occurs in wholesale markets.\nH. Explicit collusion requires physical meetings, while tacit collusion can be done online\nI. Explicit collusion is monitored by regulatory authorities, while tacit collusion goes undetected.\nJ. Explicit collusion is always done in secret, while tacit collusion is done openly.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **A**: Explicit collusion involves a direct, often documented or verbal agreement between firms to coordinate behavior (e.g., setting prices or output), while tacit collusion occurs when firms coordinate indirectly—e.g., by observing each other’s behavior and adjusting their own strategies accordingly—without any explicit communication or agreement.\n\nOption A correctly captures this distinction, whereas most other options are factually incorrect (e.g., B is partially right but overemphasizes formality and ignores tacit coordination via market signals; C, D, E, F, G, H, I, and J are all inaccurate or misleading).\n\nRegulatory authorities treat both forms as anti-competitive, though explicit collusion is easier to detect and prosecute due to tangible evidence.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
