    "global index": "MMLUPro_law_1518",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A husband is charged with murder in the shooting death of his brother-in-law. In the jurisdiction in which the husband is on trial for murder, the defense of insanity is an affirmative defense. The husband pleads insanity as his defense. At trial, in order to prove his defense of insanity, the husband has\n\nOptions: \nA. neither the burden of production nor the burden of persuasion.\nB. the burden of proof beyond a reasonable doubt.\nC. the burden of production.\nD. the responsibility to demonstrate a history of mental illness.\nE. the requirement to provide substantial evidence of his insanity at the time of the crime.\nF. the obligation to provide expert medical testimony.\nG. the duty to prove his innocence.\nH. the necessity to convince the jury of his mental instability.\nI. the burden of persuasion only.\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In jurisdictions where insanity is an affirmative defense, the defendant typically has the burden of production—to introduce sufficient evidence to raise the defense—and the burden of persuasion, often by a preponderance of the evidence. The husband must therefore prove his insanity, but not beyond a reasonable doubt. This means he has both the burden of production and the burden of persuasion, making the best answer the one that includes the burden of persuasion.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
