    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In criminal cases, an insanity defense is typically an affirmative defense, meaning the defendant must raise the issue and meet certain burdens. The defendant generally has the burden of production (to present sufficient evidence to raise the issue) and the burden of persuasion (to prove the defense by a preponderance of the evidence or by clear and convincing evidence, depending on jurisdiction), but **not** beyond a reasonable doubt. Option **E**—\"the requirement to provide substantial evidence of his insanity at the time of the crime\"—accurately reflects the burden of production (and often part of the persuasion), as the defendant must present credible evidence supporting the defense, including evidence of lack of capacity at the time of the act.\n\n\\boxed{E}",
