      "generated_answer": "The President's authority to enter into treaties with foreign nations is granted by the U.S. Constitution, but such treaties must be ratified by a two-thirds vote in the Senate to be valid. The subject matter—protecting endangered seal species through international cooperation—falls within the scope of legitimate foreign policy and environmental treaty-making. Therefore, the treaty would most likely be upheld under the treaty-making power requiring Senate ratification by a two-thirds majority.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
      "success": true,
