      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is **G**, as the U.S. Constitution (Article II, Section 2, Clause 2) grants the President the power to make treaties, but only with the advice and consent of two-thirds of the Senate. The described treaty—creating a permanent commission and granting enforcement powers—qualifies as a formal treaty requiring this high Senate threshold for validity. Other options incorrectly limit or misattribute the treaty-making process (e.g., to Congress as a whole, the House, states, or the Supreme Court) or confuse treaty-making with executive agreements or inherent executive powers.\n\n\\boxed{G}",
