      "generated_answer": "The brother’s cutting of timber on the section suitable for cultivation—where the land is *more valuable for cultivation than for timber*—constitutes **voluntary waste**, as it involves an affirmative act by the life tenant that diminishes the value of the remainder interest. Voluntary waste occurs when a life tenant intentionally alters the land in a way that reduces its value, especially when the land is more valuable for a different use (here, agriculture). While clearing land for agricultural use *could* be ameliorative waste in some jurisdictions, that doctrine typically applies only when improvements *increase* the land’s value *and* are reasonable; here, the question states the land is more valuable for cultivation *but* the timber was valuable, and the brother cut timber before clearing—suggesting a net loss in timber value without clear evidence of added agricultural value at the time, so voluntary waste is more appropriate.\n\n\\boxed{F}",
