      "generated_answer": "Let the greedy algorithm produce $ g(N) $ coins for total $ N $, using as many 25s, then 10s, then 1s as possible:  \n$ q_{25} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{N}{25} \\right\\rfloor $, $ r_{25} = N \\bmod 25 $,  \n$ q_{10} = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{r_{25}}{10} \\right\\rfloor $, $ r_{10} = r_{25} \\bmod 10 $,  \nso $ g(N) = q_{25} + q_{10} + r_{10} $.  \n\nAn optimal solution replaces $ k $ 10-cent coins and $ \\ell $ 1-cent coins with fewer total coins only if replacing some combination of dimes and pennies with quarters reduces coin count — i.e., when $ 10a + b = 25c + d $ with $ a + b > c + d $, minimal such improvement occurs when replacing three dimes (30c, 3 coins) and five pennies (5c, 5 coins) with one quarter and one dime (35c, 2 coins) — but more critically, for remainders mod 25, the greedy choice fails when $ r_{25} \\in \\{18,19,28,29\\} $ is impossible, yet here max remainder is 24. Known result: greedy fails exactly when $ N \\bmod 25 \\in \\{18,19,20,21,22,23,24\\} $ *and* the tens digit of the remainder is less than what’s optimal — but precise count: for each block of 25, greedy fails for 7 values: $ N \\equiv 18,19,20,21,22,23,24 \\pmod{25} $. Since $ 1000 = 40 \\times 25 $, there are $ 40 \\times 7 = 280 $ failing values. Thus successful count is $ 1000 - 280 = 720 $.\n\n\\boxed{720}",
