2,3,4}, and recursion resembles exponentiation: in fact, $ f(i,j) = 2^{j} \\cdot c_i \\bmod 5 $? Let’s compute values:\n\n$ f(0,0)=1, f(0,1)=2, f(0,2)=3, f(0,3)=4, f(0,4)=0 $  \n$ f(1,0)=2, f(1,1)=3, f(1,2)=4, f(1,3)=0, f(1,4)=1 $  \n$ f(2,0)=3, f(2,1)=0, f(2,2)=2, f(2,3)=4, f(2,4)=1 $  \n$ f(3,0)=0, f(3,1)=3, f(3,2)=4, f(3,3)=1, f(3,4)=0 $  \n$ f(4,0)=3, f(4,1)=4, f(4,2)=2, f(4,3)=4, f(4,4)=0 $\n\nWait: $ f(2,2)=2 $, $ f(3,2)=4 $, $ f(4,2)=2 $, $ f(5,2)=4 $... seems alternating 2,4 from $ i=2 $ onward. Check $ f(2,2)=2 $, $ f(3,2)=f(2,f(3,1))=f(2,3)=4 $, $ f(4,2)=f(3,f(4,1))=f(3,4)=0 $? No—$ f(4,1)=f(3,f(4,0))=f(3,3)=1 $, so $ f(4,2)=f(3,1)=3 $? Mistake earlier. Recompute carefully:\n\n- $ f(0,j) = (j+1) \\bmod 5 $  \n- $ f(1,0) = f(0,1) = 2 $  \n- $ f(1,1) = f(0,f(1,0)) = f(0,2) = 3 $  \n- $ f(1,2) = f(0,f(1,1)) = f(0,3) = 4 $  \n- $ f(1,3) = f(0,f(1,2)) = f(0,4) = 0 $  \n- $ f(1,4) = f(0,f(1,3)) = f(0,0) = 1 $  \n\n- $ f(2,0) = f(1,1) = 3 $  \n- $ f(2,1) = f(1,f(2,0)) = f(1,3) = 0 $  \n- $ f(2,2) = f(1,f(2,1)) = f(1,0) = 2 $  \n- $ f(2,3) = f(1,f(2,2)) = f(1,2) = 4 $  \n- $ f(2,4) = f(1,f(2,3)) = f(1,4) = 1 $  \n\n- $ f(3,0) = f(2,1) = 0 $  \n- $ f(3,1) = f(2,f(3,0)) = f(2,0) = 3 $  \n- $ f(3,2) = f(2,f(3,1)) = f(2,3) = 4 $  \n- $ f(3,3) = f(2,f(3,2)) = f(2,4) = 1 $  \n- $ f(3,4) = f(2,f(3,3)) = f(2,1) = 0 $  \n\n- $ f(4,0) = f(3,1) = 3 $  \n- $ f(4,1) = f(3,f(4,0)) = f(3,3) = 1 $  \n- $ f(4,2) = f(3,f(4,1)) = f(3,1) = 3 $  \n- $ f(4,3) = f(3,f(4,2)) = f(3,3) = 1 $  \n- $ f(4,4) = f(3,f(4,3)) = f(3,1) = 3 $  \n\n- $ f(5,0) = f(4,1) = 1 $  \n- $ f(5,1) = f(4,f(5,0)) = f(4,1) = 1 $  \n- $ f(5,2) = f(4,f(5,1)) = f(4,1) = 1 $  \nSo for $ i \\ge 5 $, $ f(i,2) = 1 $.  \n\nCheck $ f(5,2)=1 $, $ f(6,2)=f(5,f(6,1)) $, and $ f(6,1)=f(5,f(6,0))=f(5,f(5,1))=f(5,1)=1 $, so $ f(6,2)=f(5,1)=1 $. Thus constant 1 from $ i=5 $. Since $ 2015 \\ge 5 $, $ f(2015,2)=1 $.\n\n**Answer:** $\\boxed{1}$",
