      "generated_answer": "To double the volume of a cube (X=2), we need to construct edge length $ \\sqrt[3]{2} $, which is not constructible (not obtainable via compass and straightedge), since constructible numbers must have degree a power of 2 over ℚ, and $ [\\mathbb{Q}(\\sqrt[3]{2}):\\mathbb{Q}] = 3 $.  \nFor $ X = k^3 $, where $ k $ is rational (e.g., $ X = 8 = 2^3 $, $ 27 = 3^3 $, $ 343 = 7^3 $, $ 1331 = 11^3 $), the required edge length is $ k $ times the original, which is constructible if $ k $ is rational (and thus constructible).  \nSo for $ X \\in \\{3,17,8,27,343,1331\\} $, only $ X = 8, 27, 343, 1331 $ (the perfect cubes) yield constructible edge lengths. Hence the answer list is $[0, 0, 1, 1, 1, 1]$, corresponding to option **C**.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{C}\n$$",
