      "generated_answer": "We are given $x_n x_{n+1} = n$ for all $n \\ge 1$, and $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = 1$.  \nFrom the recurrence, $\\frac{x_n}{x_{n+1}} = \\frac{x_n^2}{n} \\to 1$, so $x_n^2 \\sim n$, i.e., $x_n \\sim \\sqrt{n}$.  \nUsing Stirling’s approximation on the product $x_1^2 = \\frac{1 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 5 \\cdots (2n-1)}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 6 \\cdots (2n)} \\cdot (2n)$ as $n\\to\\infty$, one finds $x_1^2 = \\frac{\\pi}{2}$, so $\\pi x_1^2 = \\frac{\\pi^2}{2} \\approx 4.9348$, but more carefully: actually, from $x_{n+1} = \\frac{n}{x_n}$, iterating gives $x_{2n} = \\frac{(2n-1)!!}{(2n-2)!!} \\cdot \\frac{1}{x_1}$, and asymptotics yield $x_1^2 = \\frac{2}{\\pi}$, so $\\pi x_1^2 = 2$.  \nThus the correct answer is $\\boxed{B}$.",
