    "global index": "MathQA_436",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: a 12 meter long wire is cut into two pieces . if the longer piece is then used to form a perimeter of a square , what is the probability that the area of the square will be more than 4 if the original wire was cut at an arbitrary point ?\n\nOptions: \nA. 1 / 6\nB. 2 / 3\nC. 3 / 10\nD. 1 / 3\nE. 2 / 5\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b-2507",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "Let the wire be cut at a point $ x $ meters from one end, where $ 0 < x < 12 $. Without loss of generality, assume the longer piece is $ \\max(x, 12 - x) $. Since the wire is 12 meters, the longer piece is at least 6 meters. This longer piece forms the perimeter of a square, so the side length is $ \\frac{\\text{longer piece}}{4} $, and the area is $ \\left(\\frac{\\text{longer piece}}{4}\\right)^2 $. We want the area $ > 4 $, so:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{L}{4}\\right)^2 > 4 \\Rightarrow \\frac{L^2}{16} > 4 \\Rightarrow L^2 > 64 \\Rightarrow L > 8\n$$\n\nSo, the longer piece must be greater than 8 meters. This happens when the cut is such that either $ x > 8 $ or $ x < 4 $. The interval satisfying this is $ (0, 4) \\cup (8, 12) $, which has total length $ 4 + 4 = 8 $. The total possible cut points are over 12 meters, so the probability is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{8}{12} = \\frac{2}{3}\n$$\n\n\\boxed{B}",
      "success": true,
      "token_usage": {
        "input_tokens": 162,
        "output_tokens": 298,
        "total_tokens": 460
      },
      "provider": "openrouter",
      "error": null
