      "generated_answer": "The energy stored in an inductor is given by $ W = \\int_0^t i(\\tau) v(\\tau) \\, d\\tau $. The voltage across the inductor is $ v(t) = \\frac{d\\phi}{dt} $, and since $ \\phi(t) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3(t) $, we have $ v(t) = \\frac{d}{dt}\\left( \\frac{1}{3}(2\\sin t)^3 \\right) = \\frac{d}{dt}\\left( \\frac{8}{3}\\sin^3 t \\right) = \\frac{8}{3} \\cdot 3\\sin^2 t \\cos t = 8\\sin^2 t \\cos t $. Then the energy is $ W = \\int_0^1 i(t) v(t)\\, dt = \\int_0^1 (2\\sin t)(8\\sin^2 t \\cos t)\\, dt = 16 \\int_0^1 \\sin^3 t \\cos t\\, dt $. Let $ u = \\sin t $, $ du = \\cos t\\, dt $, so when $ t=0 $, $ u=0 $; $ t=1 $, $ u=\\sin 1 $. Then $ W = 16 \\int_0^{\\sin 1} u^3 du = 16 \\cdot \\frac{u^4}{4} \\big|_0^{\\sin 1} = 4 \\sin^4(1) $. Since $ \\sin(1) \\approx \\sin(57.3^\\circ) \\approx 0.8415 $, $ \\sin^4(1) \\approx (0.8415)^4 \\approx 0.5 $. Then $ W \\approx 4 \\cdot 0.5 = 2.00\\, \\text{J} $.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
      "success": true,
