  {
    "global index": "MMLUPro_engineering_10537",
    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Find the energy stored in an inductor from t = 0 to t = 1 whose \\textphi -icharacteristic is given by \\textphi (t) = 1/3i^3 (t) and the current through it is given by i (t) = 2 sin t.\n\nOptions: \nA. 1.00 J\nB. 0.75 J\nC. 1.50 J\nD. 4.00 J\nE. 2.50 J\nF. 3.00 J\nG. 1.25 J\nH. 2.75 J\nI. 2.00 J\nJ. 3.50 J\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The energy stored in an inductor is given by $ W = \\int_0^1 \\phi(t) \\, di $, or equivalently $ W = \\int_0^1 i(t) \\, d\\phi(t) $.  \nGiven $ \\phi(i) = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 $, we use $ d\\phi = i^2 \\, di $, so $ W = \\int_0^1 i \\cdot i^2 \\, di = \\int_0^1 i^3 \\, di = \\left[ \\frac{i^4}{4} \\right]_0^1 = \\frac{1}{4} $.  \nBut since $ i(t) = 2\\sin t $, we must express $ W = \\int_0^1 \\phi(i(t)) \\cdot \\frac{di}{dt} \\, dt = \\int_0^1 \\frac{1}{3}(2\\sin t)^3 \\cdot 2\\cos t \\, dt = \\frac{16}{3} \\int_0^1 \\sin^3 t \\cos t \\, dt $.  \nLet $ u = \\sin t $, $ du = \\cos t \\, dt $: $ \\frac{16}{3} \\int_0^{\\sin 1} u^3 \\, du = \\frac{16}{3} \\cdot \\frac{\\sin^4 1}{4} = \\frac{4}{3} \\sin^4 1 $.  \nNumerically, $ \\sin 1 \\approx 0.8415 $, so $ \\sin^4 1 \\approx 0.5 $, giving $ W \\approx \\frac{4}{3} \\cdot 0.5 = \\frac{2}{3} \\approx 0.667 $, closest to **0.75 J** (Option B).  \nHowever, an easier approach: since $ \\phi = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 $, the energy is $ W = \\int_0^{i(1)} i \\, d\\phi = \\int_0^{2\\sin 1} i \\cdot i^2 \\, di = \\int_0^{2\\sin 1} i^3 \\, di = \\frac{(2\\sin 1)^4}{4} = 4\\sin^4 1 \\approx 4 \\cdot 0.5 = 2.0 $, which matches **2.00 J (Option I)**.  \n**Correct answer is I**, because the standard energy formula for nonlinear inductors is $ W = \\int_0^i i' \\, d\\phi(i') $, and with $ \\phi = \\frac{1}{3}i^3 $, $ d\\phi = i^2 di $, so $ W = \\int_0^{i(1)} i^3 di = \\frac{i(1)^4}{4} = \\frac{(2\\sin 1)^4}{4} \\approx 2.00 $.\n\n\\boxed{I}",
