    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: A 6-pole 3-phase 60-cycle alternator has 12 slots per pole and four conductors per slot. The winding is five-sixths pitch. There are 2,500,000 maxwells (= 0.025 weber) entering the armature from each north pole, and this flux is sinusoidally distributed along the air gap. The armature coils are all connected in series. The winding is Y-connected. Determine the open-circuit emf of the alternator.\n\nOptions: \nA. 296 volts\nB. 480 volts\nC. 425 volts\nD. 350 volts\nE. 550 volts\nF. 600 volts\nG. 513 volts\nH. 250 volts\nI. 275 volts\nJ. 700 volts\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To determine the open-circuit EMF per phase ($E_{ph}$), we use the formula $E_{ph} = 4.44 \\cdot f \\cdot \\Phi \\cdot N_{ph} \\cdot k_w$. With 6 poles and 12 slots per pole, the total number of slots is 72, and with 4 conductors per slot, there are $Z = 288$ total conductors, resulting in $N_{ph} = (288 / 3) / 2 = 48$ turns per phase. The winding factor $k_w = k_p \\cdot k_d = \\sin(5/6 \\cdot 90^\\circ) \\cdot \\frac{\\sin(60^\\circ/2)}{m \\sin(60^\\circ/(2m))}$ (for 3 phases, $k_d \\approx 0.958$ and $k_p \\approx 0.966$), leading to $E_{ph} \\approx 296$ volts. Since the question asks for the EMF per phase (often implied in alternator problems unless line voltage is specified), 296 volts is the correct value.\n\n\\boxed{A}",
      "success": true,
