    "prediction": "google/gemini-3.1-flash-lite",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The total rate of heat transfer $Q$ can be expressed as the integral of the local heat flux over the surface area, $Q = \\int_0^L h_x(T_s - T) dA$. For a pipe, $dA = P dx$ (where $P$ is the perimeter), so $Q = P \\int_0^L h_x(T_s - T) dx$. By definition, $Q = h_{av} A_{total} \\Delta T_{av}$, where $A_{total} = PL$ and $\\Delta T_{av} = (T_s - T)_{av}$; equating these expressions for $Q$ and simplifying leads to $h_{av} = \\frac{\\int_0^L h_x(T_s - T) dx}{L(T_s - T)_{av}}$. Comparing this to the given options, the most consistent form is B.\n\n\\boxed{B}",
