    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "To represent 110 distinct codewords, we need the smallest integer $ n $ such that $ 2^n \\geq 110 $. Since $ 2^6 = 64 < 110 $ and $ 2^7 = 128 \\geq 110 $, **7 bits** are required.\n\nEfficiency is defined as the ratio of information capacity to the number of possible combinations: $ \\frac{110}{128} \\times 100\\% \\approx 85.9\\% $, which rounds to about **86%**, closest to **89%** (likely using $ \\log_{10} $ or rounding conventions), but more precisely, standard information-theoretic efficiency would be $ \\frac{\\log_2(110)}{7} \\times 100\\% \\approx \\frac{6.78}{7} \\times 100\\% \\approx 96.9\\% $, which matches **H: 7 bits, 97 percent**.\n\nThus, the most accurate option is **H**, as it correctly identifies the minimum bits needed (7) and reflects high efficiency relative to the full capacity of 7 bits.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{H}\n$$",
