    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: Which type of chord has all of its inversions being equivalent chords?\n\nOptions: \nA. Minor triad\nB. Diminished triad\nC. Half-diminished seventh chord\nD. Fully diminished seventh chord\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "The fully diminished seventh chord (e.g., C°⁷ = C–E♭–G♭–B𝄫) has the property that every inversion is enharmonically equivalent to the original chord because it is constructed from stacked minor thirds, making all four notes spaced equally (three semitones apart). Thus, each inversion produces the same chord quality (a diminished seventh) with the same intervals, only with a different root. This symmetry does not hold for other chords listed.\n\n\\boxed{D}",
