    "prompt": "Please read the following multiple-choice questions and provide the most likely correct answer based on the options given.\n\nContext: None\n\nQuestion: In harmonic minor scales, which degree forms a major-minor seventh, minor-minor seventh, half-diminished seventh, or fully-diminished seventh chord?\n\nOptions: \nA. I (1st) degree\nB. III (3rd) degree\nC. VI (6th) degree\nD. II (2nd) degree\n\n\nProvide the correct letter choice in \\boxed{X}, where X is the correct letter choice. Keep the explanation or feedback within 3 sentences.",
    "prediction": "Qwen/Qwen3-Coder-Next",
    "generated_result": {
      "generated_answer": "In a harmonic minor scale, the **VI degree** forms a **fully-diminished seventh chord** (e.g., in A harmonic minor: F–A♭–C–E♭ = F°⁷), the **II degree** forms a **half-diminished seventh chord** (e.g., B–D–F–A = Bø⁷), the **III degree** forms a **minor-minor seventh chord** (e.g., C–E♭–G–B♭ = Cm⁷), and the **I degree** forms a **major-minor seventh chord** (e.g., A–C–E–G = Amaj⁷? No—actually A–C–E–G is A minor 7; wait—harmonic minor I is minor triad + major 7th, e.g., A–C–E–G♯ = A minor-major 7th).  \nBut the question asks which degree forms *any* of those four types—every diatonic degree in harmonic minor yields one of these four seventh chord types, and **VI is the only one that yields a fully-diminished seventh chord**, which is distinctive. However, re-examining:  \nIn A harmonic minor:  \nI = A–C–E–G♯ → minor-major 7th (not listed as an option type here),  \nII = B–D–F–A → half-diminished 7th,  \nIII = C–E♭–G–B♭ → minor-minor 7th,  \nIV = D–F–A–C → minor-minor 7th,  \nV = E–G♯–B–D → major-minor 7th,  \nVI = F–A♭–C–E♭ → fully-diminished 7th,  \nVII = G♯–B–D–F → major-minor 7th.  \nThe question says “which degree forms a major-minor seventh, minor-minor seventh, half-diminished seventh, or fully-diminished seventh chord?”—all degrees except I form those, but the **VI degree uniquely forms a fully-diminished seventh chord**, and it’s the only one among the options that consistently yields a *fully-diminished* seventh chord in harmonic minor.  \nYet the question is phrased as *which degree forms one of those types*—so multiple degrees do, but the most distinctive and commonly tested answer is **VI**, since it's the only one forming a *fully-diminished* chord, and the options include it.  \nStandard theory emphasizes that **the VI degree is the one forming a fully-diminished seventh chord in harmonic minor**, so the best answer is **C**.\n\n$$\n\\boxed{C}\n$$",
