      "generated_answer": "The correct answer is \\boxed{C}.\n\nThe Sugar Act was a revenue-generating measure intended to regulate commerce and protect the economic interests of the British Empire, similar to the purpose of protective tariffs which aim to regulate trade and raise revenue for the state. Both policies reflect mercantilist or protectionist economic goals designed to strengthen national control over trade and finance. Other options do not align with the imperialist, revenue-generating, and trade-restricting nature of the Sugar Act.",
